addRun("a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer--writer-claude-haiku-5-5--harvey-labs--codex--antitrust-competition-draft-leniency-application", {"meta": {"benchmark": "harvey-labs", "harness": "codex", "model": "qwen3.8-27b", "task_title": "Draft DOJ Antitrust Division Type I Corporate Leniency Application for Price-Fixing and Customer-Allocation Conspiracy", "axis": "minute", "set": "a-second-judge-and-a-frontier-writer", "arm": "writer-claude-haiku-5-5"}, "events": [{"id": "event_001", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_002", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_003", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_004", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_006", "status": "unasked"}, {"id": "event_007", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_008", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_010", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_009", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_011", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_012", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_013", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_014", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_015", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_016", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_017", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_018", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_019", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_020", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_021", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_022", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_023", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_024", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_025", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_026", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_027", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_028", "status": "covered"}, {"id": "event_029", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_030", "status": "missed"}, {"id": "event_031", "status": "covered"}], "questions": [{"id": "question_01", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report states in the Orient phase that the agent's plan tool call returned \"unsupported call\" (citing card 0004), that it carried on without a plan, and that it next checked its tools (pandoc present, python-docx not) and converted the .docx sources to text — matching the failure and recovery shown in card_0004 (the rejected update_plan call) and card_0006 (the agent noting the tool is unavailable and proceeding). The report does not name the tool \"update_plan\" or spell out the five steps, but it communicates the material answer: the plan call was unsupported and the agent deliberately proceeded without planning-tool support. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is a miss; the answer is plainly present in the report, in two places (the Orient phase paragraph and the later bullet).", "evidence": ["card_0004", "card_0006"]}, {"id": "question_02", "answer": "Because the Python docx library (python-docx) was not installed in the agent's environment while pandoc was available, the agent wrote both documents as markdown and converted them to .docx with pandoc.", "quote": "It checked its tools: pandoc was present, python-docx was not (card 0005).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks why the agent built its document pipeline around pandoc instead of a Python docx library. The report communicates the material trace-grounded answer in two places: the Orient phase states the agent \"checked its tools: pandoc was present, python-docx was not (card 0005)\" and converted the .docx sources to plain text via pandoc, and the limitations section states \"python-docx was not installed (card 0005). The agent wrote markdown and converted it with pandoc.\" Together these give both halves of the gold answer — the probe result (pandoc available, python-docx raising an import failure, per card_0005/card_0006) and the resulting decision to author markdown and convert with pandoc so the deliverables are real Office .docx files — matching cards 0004–0006 (card_0005 shows the which/import check with ModuleNotFoundError, card_0006 the reasoning \"No python-docx. pandoc is available. If I write markdown and convert it to docx with pandoc...\"). The report does not spell out the decision as an explicit choice motivated by the missing module beyond the causal juxtaposition, but the juxtaposition in both passages materially answers why pandoc was used instead of a Python docx library, and the reader's extraction is supported verbatim in the report.", "evidence": ["card_0004", "card_0005", "card_0006"]}, {"id": "question_03", "answer": "After the first bulk read of all ten sources came back truncated (800 characters of a 10,193-character output, pandoc capped at 700 lines), the agent converted the .docx sources to plain text under /tmp/work and then read the source documents one by one, mostly through pandoc output and cat. The per-file extraction yielded 30 reads of individual source documents, of which 24 were elided (truncated/cut off) and only 6 were not elided — by document: Internal investigation memo 7 reads/6 elided, DOJ leniency policy reference 7/5, SignalVault transcripts 4/3, Kensington forensic report 4/4, Board resolutions 3/2, Czerny interview summary and Tanaka complaint 3/3, Czerny notebook excerpts 2/1.", "quote": "The first bulk read (card 0007) is not counted: it shows 800 characters of a 10,193-character output, and the pandoc output was capped at 700 lines.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report communicates the material trace-grounded answer to \"what did it do instead\": after the combined read of all ten sources came back truncated (card 0007: output capped at 700 lines), the agent converted the .docx sources to plain text under /tmp/work (card 0009) and then read the source documents one by one, consistent with cards 0008, 0010, and 0011 (the first individual read being the internal investigation memo). The user consequence is met: a reader learns that all later reading was done against per-file plain-text copies in /tmp/work, not against the original docx files read in one batch. Note on the second prong: the report does not state the wc -l per-file line counts (483/491/373/389/287/227/153/111/89/89, 2692 total) that the gold answer records as the extraction's yield; instead it frames the outcome in terms of per-document read counts and elision (e.g., \"The records show 30 reads of individual source documents\" and \"Totals: 30 reads, 24 elided, 6 not elided\"). The line-count detail is a missing sub-detail, but the recovery the question asks about is explained specifically, with correct card citations, and nothing in the report contradicts the trace, so the category that fits is answerable.", "evidence": ["card_0007", "card_0008", "card_0009", "card_0010", "card_0011"]}, {"id": "question_04", "answer": "The agent trusted the names in the internal investigation memo, the board resolutions, and the SignalVault transcripts rather than the DOJ leniency policy reference's checklist, because that checklist contained name errors (TriState Polymers, SunCoast Materials, Luis Montez, Terrence Broussard) and region errors for Holst and Vero; the corrected names were TriState Resin Corp., SunCoast Plastics, LLC, Javier Montez, and Annette Broussard. The discrepancy was handled by documenting it: the advisory memorandum includes an appendix of source discrepancies, and the process notes record the source list, decisions, and discrepancies (card 0071).", "quote": "It used the names from the investigation memo, board resolutions, and transcripts, not from the internal policy reference's checklist. That checklist has name errors (TriState Polymers, SunCoast Materials, Luis Montez, Terrence Broussard) and region errors for Holst and Vero (card 0071). The corrected names are TriState Resin Corp., SunCoast Plastics, LLC, Javier Montez, and Annette Broussard.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report states the core of the gold answer: the agent took the names from the internal investigation memo (corroborated by board resolutions and transcripts) rather than from the DOJ leniency policy reference, whose checklist it identifies as containing the erroneous competitor names (TriState Polymers, SunCoast Materials) and individual names (Luis Montez, Terrence Broussard) plus region errors for Holst and Vero, and it gives the corrected names. It also shows how the discrepancy was handled: the report notes the process notes record \"Source list, decisions, discrepancies\" and the memo carries \"an appendix of source discrepancies,\" matching the trace in card_0028 and card_0040 (treat the memo as the authoritative primary source, adopt its names, flag the conflict in the process notes) and the decision recorded in card_0071. The report omits the agent's \"intentional trap\" framing and its later reaffirmation of the choice, but the decision and its handling plan are materially communicated and trace-grounded. The report-only reader's extraction was accurate and locatable (verified verbatim at char offsets 16463-16859 in sample_report.html).", "evidence": ["card_0024", "card_0028", "card_0040", "card_0071"]}, {"id": "question_05", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0030) is that after the first truncated read of the board-resolution/remedial-timeline/prior-consent-decree batch, the agent flagged a date conflict: the prior-consent-decree-summary says the decree \"expired on September 12, 2019,\" while the internal investigation memo describes a five-year 2014-2019 monitoring period ending with a 2019 final report — \"a date discrepancy I need to reconcile\" — and it was left open in that phase. The report does not communicate this. I searched the whole report for consent, discrepancy, reconcile, expired, September, monitoring, 2019, and 02891; the only consent-decree mention is a single fact table row: <tr><td>Prior matter<\/td><td>2014 consent decree, 4:14-cv-02891 (S.D. Tex.), expired 12 September 2019<\/td><td>Session notes (card 0071)<\/td><\/tr>, which states the expiration date as a flat recorded figure and never mentions the conflicting five-year monitoring period, any flag, or that the conflict was unresolved. The report's \"Open points\" section lists other recorded conflicts (account split, thread name, email date span, job title) but omits the consent-decree date discrepancy entirely. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct, so the report is scored as missing the material answer.", "evidence": ["card_0030"]}, {"id": "question_06", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "Card_0040 shows the agent cross-checking specific figures across the policy reference, Kensington, and the investigation memo — 12.3% overcharge with $75.3M consumer harm, ~$612M CPS affected commerce, ~$2.34B total, and firm revenues summing to ~$611M, about 34% of the ~$1.8B market — reporting them as consistent and treating them as verified numbers for the draft. The report never communicates this cross-check or its outcome. It lists some of the figures (12.3%, $612M, $2.34B, $75.3M) but attributes them to \"Kensington report (card 0071)\" as recorded, omits the revenue/market-share figures entirely, and says nothing about which sources agreed. Its only gesture at verification is the sentence \"The session notes label the key figures \"verified\" (card 0071). Every read of the Kensington report in the cards is elided (4 of 4), so the figures it supplies rest on partial output unless the full-text files show more.\" and the disclaimer \"These are the figures as the agent recorded them. This report did not recompute them or check them against full-text source files.\" A reader could not learn that the headline figures had been cross-verified across three specific sources or that they agreed, which is what the question asks. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0040"]}, {"id": "question_07", "answer": "The agent kept the conflict out of the draft leniency application entirely: it left the $40M board restitution cap and any dollar restitution figure out of the application, so the cap appears in the advisory memo only. It also recorded the tension as an open point, noting that the $40M board restitution cap is below the $75.3M central estimate of CPS harm, alongside other source conflicts recorded for the memo appendix (card 0071).", "quote": "It left the $40M board restitution cap and any dollar restitution figure out of the application (card 0071). The cap appears in the memo only.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks what the agent did with the $40M board cap vs $75.3M consumer-harm conflict during source review. The source cards show the trace: card_0035 (grep of the board resolution confirming Resolution 3's $40,000,000 aggregate cap) and card_0040 (reasoning: \"the policy reference says not to reference specific dollar figures, but I need to clarify whether that cap is $40 million or $75.3 million,\" i.e., the conflict was noted and left open). The report carries this substance: it states the agent kept the $40M cap and any dollar restitution figure out of the application and that the cap appears in the memo only, and it lists, under Open points, that \"The $40M board restitution cap is below the $75.3M central estimate of CPS harm.\" A reader of the report learns both that the conflict was noticed and that the figure was deliberately not settled in the draft, matching the trace-grounded answer. The report does not reproduce the grep action or the \"clarify which number\" phrasing, but it communicates the material answer — the tension was identified and deferred — so the answer is present, not merely a vague topic mention.", "evidence": ["card_0035", "card_0040"]}, {"id": "question_08", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks what material the agent explicitly listed as still unread at the end of the source-review chunk (SignalVault messages 19-28, czerny-interview-summary Sections VI-IX, the prior consent decree's entry date, the January-February middle of the remedial-measures timeline, internal-memo VI.A-VI.D and the IV.D affected-commerce table, the Kensington overcharge/consumer-harm sections, the Tanaka analysis) and what it said it would do next (create a process note and start drafting). The report never gives this list or next step. It only aggregates the topic: a per-document table of reads vs. elided reads (\"30 reads, 24 elided\") and the note \"Most source reads were partial (see above). Several cat outputs were cut off, and the agent split later reads by line range.\" That is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer what the agent explicitly enumerated as unread or what it planned next. Searched terms such as \"19-28\", \"VI-\", \"process note\", \"start drafting\", \"Tanaka analysis\" as unread, \"240\", \"next chunk\" all return nothing in the report. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is therefore correct.", "evidence": ["card_0046", "card_0052", "card_0056", "card_0058"]}, {"id": "question_09", "answer": "The report marks the transition with a \"Draft\" phase (Phase 4, cards 0071–0076) following the source-reading and synthesize phases. The first file the agent wrote in that phase was the process notes file, process/session-notes.md (card 0071) — written before the application and memo were drafted as markdown.", "quote": "Draft (cards 0071–0076). The agent wrote process/session-notes.md (card 0071), wrote the application and memo as markdown (cards 0073, 0075), and converted both to .docx with pandoc (card 0075).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report answers both parts of the question. It marks the transition as a phase change: the run is structured as Orient → Read sources (cards 0011–0069) → Synthesize (cards 0062, 0070) → Draft (cards 0071–0076), and the phase list says \"The agent wrote process/session-notes.md (card 0071), wrote the application and memo as markdown (cards 0073, 0075)\" — i.e., drafting opened with the intermediate process-notes file, before the two deliverables, matching card_0070's \"comprehensive grasp ... I'll write the process notes and create the two deliverables\" and card_0071's cat > /app/workspace/process/session-notes.md write. It further describes the notes' content (source list, decisions, discrepancies, verification section), consistent with the gold answer's account of the notes recording sources read, key decisions, discrepancies, and verified figures. The report does not quote the agent's literal \"comprehensive grasp\" declaration, but the transition and the first written file are carried by the report's own text; the report-only reader's extraction matches what the report actually says.", "evidence": ["card_0060", "card_0070", "card_0071"]}, {"id": "question_10", "answer": "The agent treated the names given in the investigation memo, board resolutions, and transcripts as authoritative rather than the internal policy reference's checklist. It resolved the conflicts by using the corrected names — TriState Resin Corp., SunCoast Plastics, LLC, Javier Montez, and Annette Broussard — in place of the checklist's erroneous competitor and co-conspirator names (TriState Polymers, SunCoast Materials, Luis Montez, Terrence Broussard), and by correcting the checklist's region labels for Holst and Vero.", "quote": "It used the names from the investigation memo, board resolutions, and transcripts, not from the internal policy reference's checklist. That checklist has name errors (TriState Polymers, SunCoast Materials, Luis Montez, Terrence Broussard) and region errors for Holst and Vero (card 0071). The corrected names are TriState Resin Corp., SunCoast Plastics, LLC, Javier Montez, and Annette Broussard.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report states the material trace-grounded answer to both parts of the question. It identifies what the agent treated as authoritative (the investigation memo, board resolutions, and transcripts, over the internal policy reference's Section X checklist) and how the conflicts were resolved (the checklist's four erroneous names — TriState Polymers, SunCoast Materials, Luis Montez, Terrence Broussard — were replaced by TriState Resin Corp., SunCoast Plastics, LLC, Javier Montez, and Annette Broussard, with the Holst/Vero region labels flagged as errors). This matches exactly what the agent recorded in its session notes per card_0062, card_0070, and card_0071 (\"Use authoritative names ... the policy reference's checklist contains name errors ... and region errors (Holst=Southeast, Vero=Northeast); corrected per investigation memo/board resolution/transcripts\"), which in turn matches the gold answer. The report also logs the related source conflicts (account split 11/15/12/9 vs 11/16/12/8, thread name, email span) under \"Open points.\" A minor detail — the specific corrected region labels (Central for Holst, Southeast for Vero) — is not spelled out, only that the checklist's region labels for Holst and Vero were errors; that does not undermine the substantive answer, and nothing in the report contradicts the trace.", "evidence": ["card_0062", "card_0070", "card_0071"]}, {"id": "question_11", "answer": "The agent produced the two deliverables as Word documents, writing them as markdown and converting to .docx with pandoc because python-docx was not installed. For dating, the draft DOJ leniency application is dated 10 March 2025 (the board's target date) and the advisory memorandum to General Counsel Pamela Ng is dated 6 March 2025; both use a March 2025 scenario calendar even though the session environment date was 5 October 2026, so the dates need resetting before use. For content, the application has 58 numbered paragraphs (sections I to XI, Exhibits A and B, a blank signature block), the memo has seven sections plus an appendix of source discrepancies, and the agent used corrected party names from the investigation memo, board resolutions, and transcripts rather than the name-errored internal checklist. Deliberately, the agent left the $40M board restitution cap and any dollar restitution figure out of the draft application (the cap appears in the memo only), and kept the Canadian Competition Bureau parallel application out of the DOJ application, raising it in the memo only.", "quote": "python-docx was not installed (card 0005). The agent wrote markdown and converted it with pandoc.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report materially communicates the trace-grounded answer on all three axes of the question. Form: real .docx deliverables produced by writing markdown and converting with pandoc because python-docx was missing, with the application as a letter (58 numbered paragraphs, exhibits, signature block) and the memo structured in seven sections. Dating: the application dated 10 March 2025 as \"the board's target date\" and the memo dated 6 March 2025 from Vasquez-Koh/Prescott to GC Pamela Ng, with the explicit contrast that both use a March 2025 scenario calendar while the environment date was 5 October 2026 — matching card_0071's recorded decisions. Content: the report explicitly states the two deliberate content exclusions from the DOJ application (no dollar figure / no $40M board cap in the restitution language; Canadian Competition Bureau application kept out, memo-only), which match card_0071 (and card_0070's plan). One gold element is absent: the report never mentions that the agent deliberately chose not to claim/fabricate a prior marker request and instead sought first-in-the-door status with a marker recommended only in the memo (card_0062, card_0070); \"marker\" appears in the report only as \"truncation marker\". That is a gap, but the report still conveys the material answer — form, dating, and the deliberate non-claims that were made — accurately and with card citations, and nothing in it contradicts the cards, so it is answerable rather than missing.", "evidence": ["card_0062", "card_0070", "card_0071"]}, {"id": "question_12", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks what the agent deliberately left unresolved for the client and which evidence gaps it could not close. The report materially communicates both halves, consistent with cards 0062, 0069, and 0070: it states that the advisory memorandum (addressed to the General Counsel) carries \"six open decisions due 7 March\" and singles out that \"Czerny's individual coverage is undecided\" and that \"demands for personal-device data from Holst and Vero are unresolved\"; it reports the SignalVault gap as \"214 recovered; 90-day auto-deletion; earlier messages lost\" (the pre-2021 digital-communications gap); and it records as a deliberate choice that the Canadian Competition Bureau parallel application \"is kept out\" of the application and \"raised it in the memo only.\" It does not enumerate all seven earmarked decisions (restitution wording, marker request, ACPERA posture, statute-of-limitations/tolling) or spell out that only Czerny's company-issued phone was imaged, so the coverage is condensed rather than exhaustive, but the report's own text conveys the deferral of strategic decisions to the client and the uncloseable evidence gaps. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is a miss: the relevant content is present in the \"What it produced,\" \"Choices the agent made,\" and \"Open points\" sections, so the report is scored on what it actually says.", "evidence": ["card_0062", "card_0069", "card_0070"]}, {"id": "question_13", "answer": "The agent produced the main deliverable — the DOJ leniency application, delivered as draft-leniency-application.docx — by writing it as markdown and converting it to .docx with pandoc, after finding that python-docx was not installed (pandoc was present). It carried the choice out during its Draft phase (writing the application as markdown and converting it to .docx with pandoc), then validated the resulting .docx file with a python zipfile check and a pandoc round-trip (replacing the missing `file` command), and re-generated it after its self-review edits.", "quote": "python-docx was not installed (card 0005). The agent wrote markdown and converted it with pandoc.", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report communicates the material trace-grounded answer: the agent chose to draft the main deliverable, the Type I leniency application, as Markdown rather than authoring the .docx directly, and converted it to .docx with pandoc. It states this in the Draft phase (\"wrote the application and memo as markdown ... and converted both to .docx with pandoc\"), in \"What went wrong or was limited\" (\"python-docx was not installed ... The agent wrote markdown and converted it with pandoc\"), and in the Orient phase (\"pandoc was present, python-docx was not\"), matching card_0072's decision to draft \"in Markdown, to be converted with pandoc,\" card_0073's heredoc creation of /tmp/work/leniency-application.md, and card_0075's session-notes decision that the deliverables be \"generated via pandoc from markdown.\" The report omits two details from the gold answer: the single quoted-heredoc command form and the intermediate source path /tmp/work/leniency-application.md (it names only the final .docx), and the agent's framing of the application as the \"main deliverable\" it would \"write carefully.\" Those are execution details on top of an answer the report clearly gives and its own text carries; the core approach and its carry-out are present and consistent with the cards.", "evidence": ["card_0072", "card_0073", "card_0075"]}, {"id": "question_14", "answer": "After the Synthesize phase, the agent entered its Draft phase (cards 0071-0076): it first wrote the process notes (process/session-notes.md, card 0071), then produced the main deliverables — the draft leniency application and the advisory memorandum — as markdown files and converted both to .docx with pandoc (cards 0073, 0075), because python-docx was not installed. The draft it wrote in that step contained: (1) the draft leniency application (/app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx), dated 10 March 2025 (the board's target date), with 58 numbered paragraphs, sections I to XI, Exhibits A and B, and a blank signature block, keeping the Canadian parallel application out of it; and (2) the advisory memorandum (/app/workspace/advisory-memorandum.docx), dated 6 March 2025, addressed to Pamela Ng, General Counsel, from Diana Vasquez-Koh and Nathan Prescott of Bellwether & Hale LLP, with seven sections, six open decisions due 7 March, and an appendix of source discrepancies. The session notes themselves recorded a source list, decisions, discrepancies, and (later) a verification section.", "quote": "Draft (cards 0071–0076). The agent wrote process/session-notes.md (card 0071), wrote the application and memo as markdown (cards 0073, 0075), and converted both to .docx with pandoc (card 0075).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report shows the transition from the process-notes phase to drafting: its phase summary has the Draft phase (cards 0071–0076) in which the agent wrote process/session-notes.md and then wrote the application (and memo) as markdown, consistent with card_0072's reasoning that the notes were completed and the leniency application draft is the next, main deliverable step. It also describes the draft's contents: dated March 10, 2025, sections I to XI, Exhibits A and B, and the draft-only \"NOT FOR FILING IN CURRENT FORM\" banner quoted from card_0073. The report does not carry every gold detail (the 284-line count, the exit-0 \"markdown written: 284 lines\" echo, or \"Type I ... eleven numbered sections\" phrasing) and it groups the memo with the application as produced, but it communicates the material trace-grounded answer that the agent finished the notes, moved to drafting the leniency application in the same phase, and what that draft contained.", "evidence": ["card_0072", "card_0073"]}, {"id": "question_15", "answer": "After finishing the draft leniency application (card 0073), the agent moved on to the companion advisory memorandum: it wrote the memo as markdown (card 0075) and converted it, together with the application, to .docx with pandoc (card 0075), before moving to verification and reporting. The report states the memorandum was dated 6 March 2025, addressed to Pamela Ng, General Counsel, from Diana Vasquez-Koh and Nathan Prescott of Bellwether & Hale LLP, and contained seven sections, six open decisions due 7 March, and an appendix of source discrepancies; the recorded source conflicts for that appendix were the account split (CPS 11, TriState 15, PacificPoly 12, SunCoast 9 per the SignalVault message vs. 11, 16, 12, 8 per Kensington's Table 4), the group thread name (\"Industry Coordination\" vs. \"Industry Council\"), the Czerny–Holst email date span, and the Czerny title (\"Senior VP of Sales\" vs. \"VP of Sales\").", "quote": "wrote the application and memo as markdown (cards 0073, 0075), and converted both to .docx with pandoc (card 0075).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report's phase account answers the \"what next\" part: in the Draft phase (cards 0071-0076) the agent \"wrote the application and memo as markdown (cards 0073, 0075), and converted both to .docx with pandoc (card 0075),\" with verification (cards 0077-0085) as the following phase - matching card_0074's stated plan (\"Next is the advisory memo. After that, convert both with pandoc and verify\") and card_0075's actual memo write plus pandoc conversion of both files. The \"What it produced\" section answers the memo-content part with details that match card_0075: dated 6 March 2025, addressed to Pamela Ng, General Counsel, from Diana Vasquez-Koh and Nathan Prescott of Bellwether & Hale LLP, seven sections (the memo has sections I-VII), six open decisions due 7 March (section V lists exactly six items), and a source-discrepancy appendix (section VI), which the report also enumerates in its \"Open points\" list. The report does not quote the agent's reasoning verbatim and summarizes the sections rather than naming the recommendation/first-in-the-door, six-condition eligibility table, principal-risks, and enclosures sections individually, but the trace-grounded substance of what happened and what the memo contained is communicated.", "evidence": ["card_0074", "card_0075"]}, {"id": "question_16", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The report never states how the agent resolved the cross-source discrepancies, which is the core of the question. card_0075 shows the memo's Section VI recording four reconciled inconsistencies, each with the side the draft adopted (SignalVault split 11/15/12/9 over Kensington Table 4's 11/16/12/8 while keeping Kensington's revenue estimates; \"Industry Coordination\" over \"Industry Council\"; the neutral \"2022 through 2024\" email span; and \"Vice President of Sales\" over \"Senior Vice President of Sales\"). The report gestures at the topic: it notes the memo has \"Seven sections, six open decisions due 7 March, and an appendix of source discrepancies (card 0075).\" and, in its Open points list, it reproduces the four conflicts under \"Source conflicts recorded for the memo appendix (card 0071):\" — including `<li>Account split: a February 18, 2022 SignalVault message gives CPS 11, TriState 15, PacificPoly 12, SunCoast 9. Kensington's Table 4 gives 11, 16, 12, 8.<\/li>`, `<li>Group thread name: \"Industry Coordination\" in the transcripts; \"Industry Council\" in the notebook.<\/li>`, and `<li>The Kensington report once calls Czerny \"Senior VP of Sales\"; other sources say \"VP of Sales\".<\/li>`. For three of the four items the report gives both sides but never says which side the draft chose, and it withholds even the revenue-estimate nuance. Only one resolution appears anywhere: `<li>Czerny–Holst email date span: June 2022 to November 2024 in the investigation memo; March 2022 to December 2024 in the interview summary. The agent used 2022–2024.<\/li>`. A report-only reader therefore cannot know how the draft silently chose sides on most of the conflicts — precisely the consequence the question flags. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is directionally right though overstated: the conflicts (and one resolution) are in the report, but the asked-for per-item resolutions are absent, so the answer is not materially communicated.", "evidence": ["card_0075"]}, {"id": "question_17", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report-only reader returned NOT_IN_REPORT, but that is a miss: the report does address the question. Card 0075 (turn 78) shows the agent drafting Section V, \"Open Items Requiring Your Decision (by March 7, 2025),\" deferring six strategic decisions to Pamela Ng (personal-device demand, Czerny individual-coverage posture, restitution language/cap question, Canadian parallel application, Exhibit A certification, and draft approval for the March 10 filing). The report communicates the material trace-grounded answer: it twice states that the advisory memo leaves six open decisions due 7 March, names the decision-maker (Pamela Ng), and identifies several of the deferred items by name — Czerny's individual coverage, the personal-device demands, the restitution-cap question, and the Canadian parallel application. It is incomplete relative to the gold (it does not enumerate all six items, the Exhibit A certification or final draft-approval items, the first-in-the-door confirmation, or the SignalVault auto-deletion forensics limit), but it is far more than a vague topic mention: the core of the answer — six decisions deliberately left open to the client with a March 7, 2025 deadline — is stated directly and carried by the report's own text, matching card_0075. Scored answerable; the reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is noted as an extraction miss.", "evidence": ["card_0075"]}, {"id": "question_18", "answer": "The agent drafted both documents as markdown (the application and memo, cards 0073 and 0075) and converted them to .docx with pandoc, because python-docx was not installed. The two final files are /app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx and /app/workspace/advisory-memorandum.docx. It confirmed them with a zip-level python zipfile check plus a pandoc round-trip of the .docx files (card 0083) and a heading check (card 0085); its reasoning and final message state both documents were verified.", "quote": "The agent wrote process/session-notes.md (card 0071), wrote the application and memo as markdown (cards 0073, 0075), and converted both to .docx with pandoc (card 0075).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent produced the two .docx deliverables and confirmed they landed in the workspace. The report says the agent \"wrote the application and memo as markdown (cards 0073, 0075), and converted both to .docx with pandoc (card 0075),\" which matches card_0075's chained pandoc -f markdown -t docx commands writing /app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx and /app/workspace/advisory-memorandum.docx, and names both files at their /app/workspace paths in the deliverables section. For confirmation, the report gives a \"zip-level .docx check and pandoc round-trip (card 0083), and a heading check (card 0085),\" which is genuinely trace-grounded: card_0083 opens both files by name in /app/workspace, asserts word/document.xml is present, and re-reads content via pandoc. The report omits the specific same-command ls -la | grep listing with the byte sizes (30,091 and 18,360) that card_0075 shows, but it conveys the material trace-grounded answer to both halves of the question — pandoc conversion of markdown sources and verification that the two docx files exist in /app/workspace and are valid — without any misstatement, so it is answerable.", "evidence": ["card_0075", "card_0083"]}, {"id": "question_19", "answer": "After both deliverables were generated, the agent's verification phase (cards 0077–0085) consisted of grep presence checks, a claim-by-claim self-review (which led to two edits and a regeneration), a zip-level .docx check plus a pandoc round-trip, and a heading check. The agent specifically validated the .docx files with a python zipfile check and a pandoc round-trip because the `file` command was missing (exit 127, card 0081).", "quote": "Verify (cards 0077–0085). Grep presence checks (cards 0077, 0079), a claim-by-claim self-review (card 0080), two edits and a regeneration (card 0081), a zip-level .docx check and pandoc round-trip (card 0083), and a heading check (card 0085).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0076) is that after both deliverables were generated the agent deliberately entered a verification phase with a two-part approach: round-trip both docx files through pandoc to confirm document validity, and cross-check key facts (names, dates, figures) against the sources. The report communicates that substance. Its Verify phase entry names both parts of the plan as executed: a \"zip-level .docx check and pandoc round-trip\" (document validity) and the fact cross-checking carried out as grep presence checks plus a claim-by-claim self-review, with the two resulting edits. It also gives the trace-grounded reason for the particular validity check: the `file` command was missing (exit 127, card_0081), so the agent validated the .docx files with a python zipfile check and a pandoc round-trip (card_0083). The report does not quote card_0076's \"two-part plan\" framing verbatim, but the approach and its why are materially present and match the cards, so the report-only reader's extraction is supported.", "evidence": ["card_0076", "card_0081", "card_0083"]}, {"id": "question_20", "answer": "The report describes the verification phase (cards 0077–0085) as running grep presence checks, a claim-by-claim self-review, two edits and a regeneration, a zip-level .docx check plus a pandoc round-trip, and a heading check. The first pass's concrete findings were: grep checks for several full names found zero matches (the agent attributed this to short-form usage, but the report notes that explanation was never checked separately against the text), the self-review found a curly-apostrophe mismatch in \"Rossi's\" attributed to pandoc typography, and the self-review found that paragraph 50's \"no contact since January 13\" claim was broader than the sources. The agent carried the discrepancies into follow-up checks: it narrowed the paragraph 50 contact claim and rewrote a memo sentence that had wrongly attributed a Texas privacy-law point to Vero (changing it to Texas and Ohio state privacy law), then regenerated the documents (card 0081).", "quote": "Verify (cards 0077–0085). Grep presence checks (cards 0077, 0079), a claim-by-claim self-review (card 0080), two edits and a regeneration (card 0081), a zip-level .docx check and pandoc round-trip (card 0083), and a heading check (card 0085).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0077) is that the first verification pass ran cleanly: the pandoc round-trip of both docx files printed \"ROUND-TRIP OK\" (271 lines application, 114 memo), a 48-term grep fact check printed per-document occurrence counts, most key facts were confirmed present in the application, \"Rossi's\" appeared 0 times in both documents, most person/figure terms showed memo=0, and the agent carried those results into follow-up checks (e.g., the re-grep for \"Rossi\" in card_0079).\n\nThe report does not communicate any of this. It never says the round-trip passed or shows any per-document occurrence counts (the report text contains no \"ROUND-TRIP OK\", line counts, or per-term app=/memo= counts; its only round-trip mention is placed under the later card 0083 check). It does not say the first pass confirmed the key facts' presence, and it never mentions the zero \"Rossi's\" count from the first-pass grep or the follow-up re-grep at all (its only \"Rossi's\" mention is the separate curly-apostrophe finding from the card 0080 self-review). The only first-pass fragment it conveys is that \"Grep checks for several full names found zero matches (cards 0077 to 0079)\", attributed to short-form usage (card 0078) — a real but narrow slice of the pass's results, not the material answer.\n\nThe report gestures at the topic without answering it: the phase list says \"Grep presence checks (cards 0077, 0079), a claim-by-claim self-review (card 0080), two edits and a regeneration (card 0081), a zip-level .docx check and pandoc round-trip (card 0083), and a heading check (card 0085)\", and the choices section says \"Grep checks for several full names found zero matches (cards 0077 to 0079). The agent attributed these to short-form usage (card 0078). The records do not show that explanation checked separately against the text.\" Neither conveys what the first pass showed (successful round-trip, confirmed facts, the zero \"Rossi's\" and memo=0 pattern) or which results drove the follow-up \"Rossi\" re-grep. A reader following the report cannot recover the trace-grounded answer, so this is a vague-topic omission rather than a wrong or unsupported account: missing_from_report, score 0.", "evidence": ["card_0077", "card_0078", "card_0079"]}, {"id": "question_21", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent explained the zero grep counts, how it re-checked, and what it decided to do about them. The report only gestures at this topic: it says the zeros were attributed to short-form usage (\"Grep checks for several full names found zero matches (cards 0077 to 0079). The agent attributed these to short-form usage (card 0078). The records do not show that explanation checked separately against the text.\") and that the self-review found a curly-apostrophe mismatch in \"Rossi's\" attributed to pandoc typography. It also mentions \"Grep presence checks (cards 0077, 0079)\" in the Verify phase summary. These are topic mentions, not the answer: the report omits the re-check itself (the flexible \"Rossi\" grep against the plain-text app output finding it on lines 67, 117, 217 with the curly apostrophe, plus the per-term count sweep in card_0079, e.g. TriState=4, Czerny=15, Holst=8, 99.2/342/214/Upjohn=0), and it omits the decision entirely (card_0080 shows the agent judging the omissions acceptable — the memo doesn't need the names of all co-conspirators and refers to the investigation broadly — and accepting the curly apostrophe as a harmless pandoc conversion, making no changes on that basis). The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT verdict is correct: the material trace-grounded answer is absent.", "evidence": ["card_0078", "card_0079", "card_0080"]}, {"id": "question_22", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer has two parts: why the rewrite was made (during paragraph-by-paragraph fact checking, the \"Since January 13, 2025, CPS has not bid on any account pursuant to the allocation...\" representation was judged plausible but not directly supported by the sources, which support the suspension, phone seizure, bid-review protocol, and a last overt act around December 2024) and what the rewrite said (anchor on last known participation in or around December 2024, state no employee has acted in furtherance since the January 2025 discovery, and cite the bid-review protocols implemented February 21, 2025 requiring Legal Department pre-approval for every bid over $50,000). Card_0080 records the self-review reasoning and the decision to soften; card_0081 records the exact old/new text applied via a Python replace script and pandoc regeneration. The report addresses this only in one bullet: it names the problem (the claim was broader than the sources) and says the agent \"narrowed it (card 0081)\". It gives none of the substance of the reason (plausible-but-unsupported representation, December 2024 last overt act) and nothing at all about what the rewrite said — the report contains no December 2024 anchoring, no \"action in furtherance\" language, and no mention of the February 21, 2025 bid-review protocols, Legal Department approval, or $50,000 threshold. A one-line \"it was broader than the sources, so it was narrowed\" is a vague topic mention that does not materially answer the question, especially the \"what did the rewrite say\" half, so this is missing_from_report. The report-only reader returned NOT_IN_REPORT, which I largely agree with, though it missed this thin bullet, quoted above, which gestures at the event without answering it.", "evidence": ["card_0080", "card_0081"]}, {"id": "question_23", "answer": "The agent changed the sentence because, on self-review, it found the memo had attributed a Texas privacy-law point to Vero, which the sources did not support. The sentence was rewritten to refer to Texas and Ohio state privacy law, since Mr. Holst (the subject of the personal-device seizure demands) is based in Ohio.", "quote": "A memo sentence attributed a Texas privacy-law point to Vero, which the sources do not support. It was rewritten to refer to Texas and Ohio state privacy law, since Mr. Holst is based in Ohio (card 0081).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The report states the reason the agent changed the memo's privacy-law sentence: the per-person attribution (a Texas privacy-law point tied to Vero) was not supported by the sources, so the sentence was rewritten to refer to Texas and Ohio state privacy law, with the retained note that Mr. Holst is based in Ohio. Card_0080 shows the agent's self-review reasoning (Vero is only located as \"Southeast,\" possibly Georgia, so attributing Texas to her was an unsupported inference; fix the line), and card_0081 shows the exact old/new strings, including the final \"(Mr. Holst is based in Ohio)\" wording. The report slightly compresses the detail (the original line also tied Texas to Czerny) but conveys the material trace-grounded answer.", "evidence": ["card_0080", "card_0081"]}, {"id": "question_24", "answer": "At the end of the edit-and-regenerate step (card 0081), the `file` command was missing and failed with exit 127. The agent recovered by validating the regenerated .docx files with a python zipfile check and a pandoc round-trip (card 0083), followed by a heading check (card 0085).", "quote": "The file command was missing (exit 127, card 0081). The agent validated the .docx files with a python zipfile check and a pandoc round-trip (card 0083).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks what failed at the end of the edit-and-regenerate command and how the agent recovered. Card_0081 shows the command ending with a `file` call that failed with \"file: command not found\" (exit 127) after \"edits applied\" and both pandoc docx regenerations succeeded; card_0082 shows the agent deciding to verify the docx as a zip archive; card_0083 shows the zipfile assertion on word/document.xml plus pandoc round-trip greps confirming the deliverables. The report's \"What went wrong or was limited\" section states exactly that: the file command was missing (exit 127, card 0081) and the agent validated the .docx files with a python zipfile check and a pandoc round-trip (card 0083), with the Verify summary reiterating the zip-level .docx check and pandoc round-trip. Both failure and recovery are communicated, matching the trace.", "evidence": ["card_0081", "card_0082", "card_0083"]}, {"id": "question_25", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (card_0083) has two parts: (1) the final round-trip verified both files were valid docx (word/document.xml of 86,386 and 39,554 chars) and, via pandoc plain-text greps, that the application contains \"bid-review protocols implemented\" (1 hit) and \"December 2024\" (5 hits) and the memo contains the fixed \"Holst is based in Ohio\" sentence; (2) the application was confirmed to contain neither the $40M cap figure nor any Canadian Competition Bureau reference. The report only answers the second half, and even then attributes it to the agent's drafting choices (card 0071) rather than the round-trip verification: \"It left the $40M board restitution cap and any dollar restitution figure out of the application (card 0071). The cap appears in the memo only.\" and \"It kept the Canadian Competition Bureau parallel application out of the DOJ application and raised it in the memo only (card 0071).\" For the first half it merely gestures at the topic without stating what the round-trip verified: \"The agent validated the .docx files with a python zipfile check and a pandoc round-trip (card 0083).\" and in the phase list \"a zip-level .docx check and pandoc round-trip (card 0083)\". Nowhere does it say the files passed as valid docx, give the character counts, or report the grep hits for \"bid-review protocols implemented\", \"December 2024\", or the Holst/Ohio memo fix; the Holst rewrite is only mentioned as an earlier edit (card 0081). Since the substantive content of the integrity round-trip's results is absent, the report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT is essentially right (the report does convey the absences, but not what the round-trip verified), so this is a vague topic mention rather than a material answer.", "evidence": ["card_0083", "card_0084"]}, {"id": "question_26", "answer": "NOT_IN_REPORT", "quote": null, "read": "missing_from_report", "reads": 2, "verdict": "missing_from_report", "score": 0, "rationale": "The gold answer (cards 0085–0086) is the specific list of structural elements the final pandoc check confirmed: for the application, the PROPOSED DRAFT banner, the DOJ Antitrust Division address block (450 Fifth Street, N.W.), the March 10, 2025 date line, the Re: line, and all 11 numbered headings (I–XI) plus Exhibits; for the memo, the privileged/confidential banner, the TO/FROM/DATE/RE table, and its 7 sections (I–VII); after which the agent declared both verified. The report never gives that list. It only names the checks generically — \"a zip-level .docx check and pandoc round-trip (card 0083), and a heading check (card 0085)\" — and separately notes the agent's \"Both documents have been verified\" reasoning (card 0086). The \"What it produced\" section lists sections I to XI and Exhibits A/B for the application (citing card 0071) and seven sections for the memo (citing card 0075), but as descriptions of the deliverables, not as elements confirmed by the final pandoc check, and it omits the banner, address block, date line, Re: line, and memo addressee table entirely. A reader could not reconstruct what the final check actually confirmed. The report-only reader's NOT_IN_REPORT extraction is correct.", "evidence": ["card_0085", "card_0086"]}, {"id": "question_27", "answer": "After the verification phase, the agent finalized the session in its \"Report\" phase (cards 0086–0088): its reasoning stated \"Both documents have been verified\" (card 0086), it added a verification section to the session notes (card 0087), and it sent a final message (card 0088) claiming both documents were \"verified against all ten source documents\" — wording the report notes the records do not support.", "quote": "Report (cards 0086–0088). The agent's reasoning states \"Both documents have been verified\" (card 0086). It added a verification section to the session notes (card 0087) and sent a final message (card 0088).", "read": "complete", "reads": 1, "verdict": "answerable", "score": 1, "rationale": "The question asks how the agent finalized the session after verification passed. Card_0086 shows the agent's stated finalization plan — updating the process notes with verification results and final state — and card_0087 shows the action: appending \"Verification results (final)\" and \"Final state\" sections to /app/workspace/process/session-notes.md. The report captures this: its Report-phase bullet records the card_0086 reasoning (\"Both documents have been verified\") and states the agent \"added a verification section to the session notes (card 0087) and sent a final message (card 0088).\" Its \"What it produced\" list separately documents the process notes deliverable as containing \"Source list, decisions, discrepancies, and a verification section (cards 0071, 0087),\" tying the appended section to the session-notes file. That materially communicates the trace-grounded answer: the session closed by recording verification results (and final state) in the process notes. The report-only reader located the same passage, so no extraction miss. The report does not name the appended sections or detail their contents, and it does not say a plan was \"marked complete\" — it notes earlier that the plan tool returned \"unsupported call\" (card 0004), so no plan status existed in the records to report; that gap does not undercut the core of what the gold answer asks. The report's caveat that the final message overclaims verification is its own critical overlay, not a misstatement of how the session was finalized.", "evidence": ["card_0086", "card_0087"]}], "stages": {"spend": {"census": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "questions": {"sessions": 0, "seconds": 0}, "answer": {"sessions": 38, "seconds": 11992}, "judge": {"sessions": 27, "seconds": 18356}, "claims": {"sessions": 59, "seconds": 16166}, "recoveries": {"sessions": 2, "seconds": 211}}, "claims": [{"id": "c1", "claim": "The agent's final deliverables for the run were two Word (.docx) documents prepared for Consolidated Polymer Solutions, Inc. (CPS), drawn from ten source files: a draft U.S. Department of Justice Antitrust Division leniency application and an advisory memorandum to Pamela Ng, General Counsel. The section does not label the run complete, partial, failed or unverified.", "type": "outcome_claim", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0002 shows exactly ten source files in /app/workspace (nine .docx plus tanaka-whistleblower-complaint.eml), matching \"drawn from ten source files.\" card_0075 shows the advisory memorandum drafted \"TO: Pamela Ng, General Counsel, Consolidated Polymer Solutions, Inc.\" and accompanying a draft Type I leniency application for the Antitrust Division of the United States Department of Justice. card_0087 (final session-notes verification) confirms both outputs are valid .docx files (zip check: word/document.xml present) and lists the ten sources. card_0088, the final turn, presents the two deliverables as complete: /app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx (Type I corporate leniency appli … [364 more characters]"}, {"id": "c2", "claim": "The leniency application is a proposed draft in current form: the agent labels it \"PROPOSED DRAFT ... NOT FOR FILING IN CURRENT FORM\", and the records show no filing, sending, or signing.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0073 shows the agent writing /tmp/work/leniency-application.md with the exact banner \"# PROPOSED DRAFT — FOR CLIENT AND COUNSEL REVIEW — NOT FOR FILING IN CURRENT FORM\", matching the label the claim quotes. card_0085 confirms the banner is still the first line of the final /app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx after conversion. The claim's second half — no filing, sending, or signing in the records — matches the whole saved trajectory: every action is reading source documents, writing the draft files, and running verification checks (pandoc round-trips, docx validity, spot-checks). card_0087's session notes list only deliverable files and verification results (\"draft banner on … [251 more characters]"}, {"id": "c3", "claim": "The original task prompt is not among the run records, so the report takes the scope of the work from the deliverables and the agent's notes (card 0071).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The saved run records contain no original task prompt: the 88 cards begin at turn 4 (card_0001, an assistant observation) and the only role tags present across all cards are role=assistant and role=tool, with no user message or prompt text captured anywhere. This matches the report's stated basis that the prompt is not in the records. Card_0071 confirms the \"agent's notes\" half of the claim: it writes /app/workspace/process/session-notes.md containing the session's scope (sources, key decisions, discrepancies), and card_0088 shows the deliverables (draft-leniency-application.docx, advisory-memorandum.docx) that together define the scope of the work."}, {"id": "c4", "claim": "The agent's verification was partial.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim says the agent's verification was partial (unresolved work), but the trace shows a thorough, closed-loop verification pass. card_0077 shows a pandoc round-trip of both deliverables plus ~49 name/date/figure grep fact-checks (ROUND-TRIP OK, counts printed for every term); card_0079 shows the agent re-running the memo checks with the correct shortened terms after noticing zero counts, closing that gap; card_0081 shows the two issues the checks surfaced (overbroad para-50 termination language, misattributed privacy-law sentence) being edited and the docx files re-rendered; card_0083 shows the final structural validation (zipfile word/document.xml checks, round-trip re-verify of the ed … [342 more characters]"}, {"id": "c5", "claim": "Most source reads were cut off (their outputs were elided), so the reads did not produce complete output.", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The saved source reads show widespread runtime output truncation, and the agent's own observations confirm the reads were cut off. Of the source_inspection cards in the trace, 25 carry the \"Warning: truncated output\" runtime marker (card_0007, card_0011, card_0013, card_0015, card_0017, card_0019, card_0021, card_0023, card_0025, card_0029, card_0031, card_0033, card_0037, card_0041, card_0043, card_0045, card_0049, card_0051, card_0053, card_0055, card_0057, card_0059, card_0061, card_0065, card_0067), versus roughly 10 untruncated source reads, several of which (e.g. card_0071, card_0073, card_0075, card_0087) are file writes rather than reads. For example, card_0007 shows the batch pandoc … [801 more characters]"}, {"id": "c6", "claim": "The agent's final message claims more verification than the records support.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The report claims the agent's final message \"claims more verification than the records support.\" Card_0088 contains the final message, whose verification claims are specific and checkable: both files valid Office documents (zip + word/document.xml), clean pandoc round-trip, ~50 spot-checks of names/dates/figures passed, no $40M cap and no Canadian Competition Bureau reference in the application, 11 sections + exhibits. The records show that verification actually happened and passed: card_0083 shows the zipfile/document.xml asserts plus the cap/Canadian greps returning 0 (\"no cap figure in app (good)\", \"no competition-bureau reference in app (good)\"); card_0077 shows \"ROUND-TRIP OK\" with non- … [781 more characters]"}, {"id": "c7", "claim": "The agent's run consisted of six phases in order: Phase 1 Orient (cards 0001-0010), Phase 2 Read sources (0011-0069), Phase 3 Synthesize (0062, 0070), Phase 4 Draft (0071-0076), Phase 5 Verify (0077-0085), and Phase 6 Report (0086-0088).", "type": "task_transition", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trajectory matches the six-phase structure in order. Phase 1 Orient (0001-0010): card_0001 opens with \"explore the workspace,\" card_0004 is the update_plan call laying out read/draft/verify steps, card_0005/card_0007/card_0009 (index) check tooling and convert the ten .docx sources to /tmp/work text, and card_0010 declares \"Let's read the full text file now.\" Phase 2 Read sources (0011-0069): the index shows this entire span is alternating source_inspection reads of the ten converted documents (cat/sed/grep over /tmp/work/*.txt) with observation cards in between. Phase 3 Synthesize: card_0062 (turn 65) is pure synthesis reasoning — \"I've now got a comprehensive grasp of the overall pictu … [1,291 more characters]"}, {"id": "c8", "claim": "The agent's plan tool call failed, returning \"unsupported call\" (card 0004).", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0004 (turn 7) shows the agent invoking the update_plan tool with a five-step plan, and the tool result is exactly \"unsupported call: update_plan\", matching the claim that the plan tool call failed and returned \"unsupported call\"."}, {"id": "c9", "claim": "After the plan tool failed, the agent carried on without a plan.", "type": "recovery", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0004 shows the update_plan call at turn 7 returning \"unsupported call: update_plan\" (the plan tool failed). card_0006 shows the agent immediately acknowledging this (\"The update_plan tool is not available in this environment (I tried to call it but it wasn't supported). I'll proceed without it\") and moving on to reading the source documents, and the rest of the trajectory (88 cards) contains no further plan calls, so the agent carried on without a plan."}, {"id": "c10", "claim": "Listing the workspace showed ten source files (card 0002).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0002 shows the agent running `find /app/workspace -type f` plus `ls -la /app/workspace`; the find output lists exactly ten files (nine .docx and tanaka-whistleblower-complaint.eml), and the full ls -la output (cards/full/card_0002.txt) lists those same ten entries with no others. card_0004 corroborates, with the agent's first plan step \"Read all 10 source documents in workspace\"."}, {"id": "c11", "claim": "The agent's tool check found that pandoc was present and python-docx was not (card 0005).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0005 shows the tool check: `which pandoc python3` returned /usr/bin/pandoc (pandoc present), and `python3 -c \"import docx; print('python-docx ok')\"` failed with ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'docx', i.e. python-docx was not installed. card_0006 corroborates the agent's own reading of that check (\"No python-docx. pandoc is available\")."}, {"id": "c12", "claim": "The agent converted the .docx source files to plain text under /tmp/work (card 0009).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0009 shows the agent running `cd /app/workspace && for f in *.docx; do pandoc \"$f\" -t plain --wrap=none > \"/tmp/work/${f%.docx}.txt\"; done` (exit code 0), with the `wc -l` evidence listing the resulting plain-text files under /tmp/work (e.g. internal-investigation-memo.txt 483 lines, doj-leniency-policy-reference.txt 287 lines, plus seven more, 2692 total). card_0010 shows the agent immediately proceeding to read those /tmp/work .txt files, consistent with the conversion having succeeded."}, {"id": "c13", "claim": "The run moved to the Read sources phase (cards 0011-0069), in which the agent read the source documents one by one, mostly through pandoc output and cat.", "type": "task_transition", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0004 shows the plan step \"Read all 10 source documents in workspace\" set to in_progress, marking the transition into the read-sources phase. card_0009 shows the sources converted to text via pandoc into /tmp/work/*.txt (10 files). From card_0011 (cat /tmp/work/internal-investigation-memo.txt, prefaced by card_0010's reasoning \"Let's read the full text file now\"), the index shows cards 0011-0069 are a sequential run of source_inspection/observation cards reading those pandoc-output files, document by document: the memo (0011-0021), doj-leniency-policy-reference (0023-0027), board-resolution/remedial-measures/prior-consent-decree (0029), tanaka/czerny-interview (0031), kensington-forensic … [411 more characters]"}, {"id": "c14", "claim": "The Read sources phase consisted of 30 per-document reads.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The index and the inspected cards show the Read sources phase (after the plan step \"Read all 10 source documents in workspace\" in card_0004 and the batch pandoc conversion in card_0009) consisted of exactly 30 per-document read commands: cards 0011, 0013, 0015, 0017, 0019, 0021 (memo), 0023, 0025, 0027 (DOJ leniency), 0029, 0031, 0033, 0035 (board/whistleblower/resolutions), 0037 (Kensington), 0039, 0041 (Czerny notebook), 0043, 0045, 0047, 0049 (SignalVault/remedial), 0051, 0053 (Czerny interview), 0055, 0057, 0059 (consent decree/Kensington), 0061, 0063, 0065, 0067 (DOJ leniency), and 0069 (memo) — (69-11)/2+1 = 30, all tagged source_inspection with cat/sed/grep on individual document file … [484 more characters]"}, {"id": "c15", "claim": "24 of the 30 per-document read outputs were elided (cut off).", "type": "runtime_failure", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trace contains exactly 30 document-read commands (cat/sed/grep reads of the\nten /tmp/work plain-text documents, turns 14-72, cards 0011, 0013, 0015, 0017,\n0019, 0021, 0023, 0025, 0027, 0029, 0031, 0033, 0035, 0037, 0039, 0041, 0043,\n0045, 0047, 0049, 0051, 0053, 0055, 0057, 0059, 0061, 0063, 0065, 0067, 0069).\nOf these, 24 outputs carry the harness elision warning \"Warning: truncated\noutput\" (e.g. card_0011, cat of the 10165-char internal-investigation-memo,\ntruncated at 800 chars), and 6 do not (card_0027, card_0035, card_0039,\ncard_0047, card_0063, card_0069) - so exactly 24 of 30 reads were elided.\ncard_0008 shows the agent itself observing \"The output is cut off partway\nthrough\" and … [242 more characters]"}, {"id": "c16", "claim": "In the Synthesize phase (cards 0062, 0070), two long reasoning briefs recorded the key facts, the source conflicts, and the plan for the memo.", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "Card_0062 (turn 65) and card_0070 (turn 73) are the two largest reasoning blocks in the trace (14,855 and 31,744 evidence chars), matching the claim's \"two long reasoning briefs.\" Both record the same three things the claim names: card_0062 opens with a numbered \"Inconsistencies across sources\" list (competitor company names, co-conspirator names, Holst/Vero titles, Kensington vs. SignalVault account splits) and works through how to handle each; card_0070 opens with a \"Key facts to use\" section (CPS corporate facts, employees, revenue), restates the numbered source conflicts, and lays out the plan for the deliverables, including an explicit \"Advisory memo structure\" outline plus conventions, … [65 more characters]"}, {"id": "c17", "claim": "In the Draft phase (cards 0071-0076), the agent wrote process/session-notes.md (card 0071).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0071 (turn 74) shows the agent's exact command: `cat > /app/workspace/process/session-notes.md << 'EOF' ...`, a full heredoc of process notes (sources read, decisions, discrepancies, verified figures), and its evidence output is \"written\", confirming the file was created. card_0072's reasoning immediately after states \"The initial process notes have been completed\", corroborating the write. The claim's card attribution (card 0071) and location within the draft-phase span (0071-0076) match the index."}, {"id": "c18", "claim": "In the Draft phase, the agent wrote the application and the memo as markdown (cards 0073, 0075).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0073 (turn 76) shows the agent writing the draft application with `cat > /tmp/work/leniency-application.md << 'MDEOF' ...`, i.e., as a markdown file; card_0075 (turn 78) shows the advisory memo written the same way to /tmp/work/advisory-memo.md. The surrounding draft-phase context (card_0071 session notes describing \"drafting\" deliverables generated via pandoc from markdown) matches the \"Draft phase\" framing, so the claim that the agent wrote the application and memo as markdown is accurately supported."}, {"id": "c19", "claim": "In the Draft phase, the agent converted both markdown documents to .docx with pandoc (card 0075).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0075 shows the agent writing the two markdown drafts (leniency-application.md, advisory-memo.md) and then running `pandoc ... -f markdown -t docx` for both, producing /app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx and /app/workspace/advisory-memorandum.docx; the card's own evidence lists both .docx files created. card_0083 confirms both files are valid docx with expected content, corroborating that the conversion happened as stated. The conversion is part of producing the draft deliverables (the \"Draft phase\" documents), consistent with the claim."}, {"id": "c20", "claim": "In the Verify phase, the agent ran grep presence checks (cards 0077, 0079).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0077 shows the Verify phase round-tripping the draft docx files to plain text and running grep -c presence checks for ~48 named terms/entities against verify-app.txt and verify-memo.txt (output \"=== fact checks ===\" with per-term counts). card_0079 shows a follow-up grep -n for \"Rossi\" and a second batch of grep -c presence checks against verify-memo.txt. Both are grep presence checks in the verification step, exactly as the claim states."}, {"id": "c21", "claim": "In the Verify phase, the agent did a claim-by-claim self-review (card 0080).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0079 shows the agent running grep checks over the extracted verify-app.txt / verify-memo.txt during the Verify phase, and card_0080 (the card the claim names) contains the agent's reasoning walking through the deliverables paragraph by paragraph and claim by claim — \"Application ¶56 … Good.\", \"Application ¶41: Holst … consistent.\", \"Application ¶52(d) … Good.\", \"Memo checks: …\", \"Memo II Condition 3 table … Good\" — comparing each factual assertion against the source documents and flagging two items to fix (the state-attribution line in memo III.B.2 and the rewrite of Application ¶50). That is exactly a claim-by-claim self-review of the drafted content, as the claim states."}, {"id": "c22", "claim": "In the Verify phase, the agent made two edits and regenerated the documents (card 0081).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0081 (roles: verification, turn 84) shows the agent running a single script that applies exactly two text replacements — one in leniency-application.md (para 50 termination language) and one in advisory-memo.md (privacy-law sentence) — prints \"edits applied\", then regenerates both documents via pandoc into /app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx and /app/workspace/advisory-memorandum.docx. card_0083 confirms the regenerated files are valid .docx (document.xml present, 86386/39554 chars) and that the edits round-tripped, so the two edits and regeneration the claim attributes to card 0081 happened as stated."}, {"id": "c23", "claim": "In the Verify phase, the agent ran a zip-level .docx check and a pandoc round-trip (card 0083).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0083 (turn 86) shows exactly the two checks the claim names: (1) a zip-level .docx check via python3 zipfile, asserting 'word/document.xml' is in the archive and printing the document.xml length for both draft-leniency-application.docx (86386 chars) and advisory-memorandum.docx (39554 chars); and (2) pandoc round-trips of both .docx files to plain text (pandoc ... -t plain --wrap=none) with grep counts verifying key content (\"bid-review protocols implemented\": 1, \"December 2024\": 5, memo \"Holst is based in Ohio\": 1) and confirming no placeholder/cap leaks. The command succeeded with expected output, and card_0084's reasoning confirms the agent treated both files as valid docx and procee … [105 more characters]"}, {"id": "c24", "claim": "In the Verify phase, the agent ran a heading check (card 0085).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0085 (turn 88) runs pandoc over draft-leniency-application.docx and advisory-memorandum.docx and greps for top-level roman-numeral headings (e.g. \"I. Introduction\", \"II. Identification of the Applicant\"), i.e. a heading check; its output lists the found section headings. The surrounding cards (chunk 7-8 verification steps, and card_0086's \"Both documents have been verified\") place this in the Verify phase, matching the claim."}, {"id": "c25", "claim": "In the Report phase (cards 0086-0088), the agent's reasoning states \"Both documents have been verified\" (card 0086).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0086 (turn 89, in the final verification/report span alongside card_0087/card_0088) contains the agent's reasoning verbatim: \"Both documents have been verified. Updating the process notes with the verification results and final state, and marking the plan as complete.\" card_0087 confirms this is the reporting/verification stage, as it appends the final verification results for both docx outputs to session-notes.md. The quoted reasoning and its attribution to card 0086 match the saved evidence exactly."}, {"id": "c26", "claim": "In the Report phase, the agent added a verification section to the session notes (card 0087).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0087 (turn 90) shows the agent appending a \"## Verification results (final)\" section, plus a \"## Final state\" summary, to /app/workspace/process/session-notes.md via a cat >> heredoc; the command completed successfully (\"done\"). The appended section contains verification results (docx zipfile checks, pandoc round-trip, ~50 spot-checks), exactly matching the claimed \"verification section added to the session notes.\""}, {"id": "c27", "claim": "In the Report phase, the agent sent a final message (card 0088).", "type": "other", "section": 1, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0088 (turn 91, the final turn of the trace) contains an assistant message with the reasoning \"Both deliverables are complete and verified. Let me summarize for the user,\" followed by the full final summary of both deliverables and the verification performed. This is exactly the claimed final message, sent after the verification work, i.e., in the Report phase."}, {"id": "c28", "claim": "The agent produced a draft leniency application at /app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx, dated 10 March 2025 (the board's target date), with 58 numbered paragraphs, sections I to XI, Exhibits A and B, and a blank signature block; the Canadian parallel application is kept out of it.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0073 shows the agent writing the draft application markdown: dated \"March 10, 2025\", numbered paragraphs running through \"58. CPS self-discovered...\", sections I through XI, an Exhibits section listing Exhibit A (certified board resolutions) and Exhibit B (documentary evidence index), and a blank signature block (\"By: ______\" followed by Diana Vasquez-Koh, Partner / Nathan Prescott, Senior Associate, with no signature entered). card_0085 shows the generated /app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx round-tripping via pandoc with the header \"Date: March 10, 2025\" and section headings I. Introduction through XI. Conclusion plus \"Exhibits\". card_0087's final verification notes confirm … [659 more characters]"}, {"id": "c29", "claim": "The agent produced an advisory memorandum at /app/workspace/advisory-memorandum.docx, dated 6 March 2025, addressed to Pamela Ng, General Counsel, from Diana Vasquez-Koh and Nathan Prescott of Bellwether & Hale LLP, with seven sections, six open decisions due 7 March, and an appendix of source discrepancies.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0075 writes the memo markdown and converts it to /app/workspace/advisory-memorandum.docx; its content matches every element of the claim: DATE \"March 6, 2025\", TO \"Pamela Ng, General Counsel\", FROM \"Diana Vasquez-Koh, Partner; Nathan Prescott, Senior Associate, Bellwether & Hale LLP\", exactly seven sections (I–VII: Recommendation; Type I Eligibility; Principal Risks; ...; Open Items; Source Discrepancies; Enclosures), \"V. Open Items Requiring Your Decision (by March 7, 2025)\" containing six numbered items, and section VI \"Source Discrepancies Identified During Drafting (For the Record)\" with four listed discrepancies. card_0083 confirms advisory-memorandum.docx is a valid docx (document … [227 more characters]"}, {"id": "c30", "claim": "The agent produced process notes at /app/workspace/process/session-notes.md containing a source list, decisions, discrepancies, and a verification section.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0071 shows the agent writing /app/workspace/process/session-notes.md via heredoc, containing \"## Sources read\" (the 10-source list), \"## Decisions,\" \"## Source discrepancies noted,\" and \"## Key figures verified,\" and the command printed \"written\". card_0087 shows it appending \"## Verification results (final)\" and \"## Final state\" to the same file (command printed \"done\"). Together the file contains a source list, decisions, discrepancies, and a verification section, exactly as claimed."}, {"id": "c31", "claim": "The run's records show 30 reads of individual source documents.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The index lists exactly 30 consecutive source_inspection turns (cards 0011-0069, odd ordinals 11 through 69), and every one of those commands reads an individual source document in /tmp/work (cat/sed/grep/awk on internal-investigation-memo.txt, doj-leniency-policy-reference.txt, board-resolution-leniency.txt, remedial-measures-timeline.txt, prior-consent-decree-summary.txt, tanaka-whistleblower-complaint.txt, czerny-interview-summary.txt, kensington-forensic-report.txt, czerny-notebook-excerpts.txt, signalvault-transcripts.txt). Earlier cards are workspace-level (card_0002 find, card_0007 pandoc of all docx, card_0009 conversion) and later source_inspection cards (0071+) are writes/verificat … [444 more characters]"}, {"id": "c32", "claim": "24 of the 30 source reads were elided or cut off, so the corresponding documents were not read in full.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The 30 source-document reads (cat/sed/grep over the converted /tmp/work/*.txt files) split exactly 24/6 as claimed. The 24 cards whose tool output carried the harness \"Warning: truncated output\" marker (card_0011, 0013, 0015, 0017, 0019, 0021, 0023, 0025, 0029, 0031, 0033, 0037, 0041, 0043, 0045, 0049, 0051, 0053, 0055, 0057, 0059, 0061, 0065, 0067) each show only a partial slice of the document, while the other 6 reads (card_0027, 0035, 0039, 0047, 0063, 0069) completed without truncation. card_0011 shows the full-file cat of the internal investigation memo cut off, and card_0012 records the agent's own reasoning: \"The output got truncated. To check the middle part, I'll split up the intern … [280 more characters]"}, {"id": "c33", "claim": "Reads per document: internal investigation memo 7 (6 elided), DOJ leniency policy reference 7 (5 elided), SignalVault transcripts 4 (3 elided), Kensington forensic report 4 (4 elided), board resolutions 3 (2 elided), Czerny interview and Tanaka complaint 3 (3 elided), Czerny notebook excerpts 2 (1 elided); total 30 reads, 24 elided.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The trace contains exactly 30 document-read commands, and the per-document\nattribution matches the claim. internal-investigation-memo.txt is read in\ncard_0011 (cat), card_0013, card_0015, card_0017, card_0019, card_0021\n(sed windows), and card_0069 (grep/sed/awk) = 7 reads; its evidence shows a\n\"Warning: truncated output\" marker on all six sed/cat cards except card_0069\n= 6 elided. doj-leniency-policy-reference.txt is read in card_0023 (cat),\ncard_0025, card_0027, card_0061, card_0063, card_0065, card_0067 = 7 reads;\nthe truncation warning appears on 0023, 0025, 0061, 0065, 0067 but not 0027\nor 0063 = 5 elided. signalvault-transcripts.txt: card_0043, card_0045,\ncard_0047, card_0049 = 4 reads … [903 more characters]"}, {"id": "c34", "claim": "The first bulk read (card 0007) shows 800 characters of a 10,193-character output, and the pandoc output was capped at 700 lines.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0007 is the first bulk read (turn 10): a for-loop over all *.docx files running pandoc -t plain, piped through head -700. Its evidence shows \"Total output lines: 700\", confirming the 700-line cap, and the card records \"The command produced 10193 characters. This card shows the first 800\" (also echoed by the \"[output truncated to 800 of 10193 chars]\" marker), matching the claim's 800-of-10,193 characterization."}, {"id": "c35", "claim": "Of the 35 full-text evidence files in cards/full, 25 contain a truncation or elision marker, and full text exists for some cards only.", "type": "other", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "input/cards/full holds exactly 35 full-text files out of 88 source cards, and exactly 25 of the 35 begin with the harness truncation marker \"Warning: truncated output...\" (verified by listing and grep over input/cards/full). card_0007 and card_0011 are two such truncated full-text files, while files like card_0002 and card_0027 are complete, confirming that full text exists for some cards only and that the 25-of-35 truncation count is accurate."}, {"id": "c36", "claim": "The session notes label the key figures \"verified\" (card 0071).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0071 shows the session notes written to /app/workspace/process/session-notes.md contain a section headed \"## Key figures verified\" followed by the key figures (market ~$1.8B, four-firm revenue $611M, affected commerce $2.34B, etc.), so the notes indeed label the key figures \"verified\" as the claim states."}, {"id": "c37", "claim": "Every read of the Kensington report in the cards is elided (4 of 4), so the figures it supplies rest on partial output unless the full-text files show more.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "A grep over the cards shows exactly four command-level reads of the Kensington report: card_0037 (cat of kensington-forensic-report.txt), card_0055 (sed 60,160p combined with the prior-consent-decree read), card_0057 (grep + sed 160,240p), and card_0059 (sed 240,330p). All four evidence blocks end with the elision marker \"[output truncated to 800 of N chars; the full text is in cards/full/]\", so the claim that every read (4 of 4) is elided is exactly right. The card files themselves point to full-text copies under cards/full/, matching the claim's own caveat \"unless the full-text files show more\". Other cards naming Kensington (0002, 0009, 0022, 0028, 0034, 0036, 0071, 0087) are only file li … [80 more characters]"}, {"id": "c38", "claim": "The agent used the names from the investigation memo, board resolutions, and transcripts, not the internal policy reference's checklist, because that checklist has name errors (TriState Polymers, SunCoast Materials, Luis Montez, Terrence Broussard) and region errors for Holst and Vero; the corrected names are TriState Resin Corp., SunCoast Plastics, LLC, Javier Montez, and Annette Broussard.", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0071 is the agent's own written decision log (session-notes.md), which records verbatim: \"Use authoritative names (TriState Resin Corp.; SunCoast Plastics, LLC; Javier Montez; Annette Broussard) — the policy reference's checklist contains name errors (TriState Polymers, SunCoast Materials, Luis Montez, Terrence Broussard) and region errors (Holst=Southeast, Vero=Northeast); corrected per investigation memo/board resolution/transcripts.\" This matches the claim's stated names, error names, error source (the policy reference checklist), and the rationale (authoritative names from the memo, board resolutions, transcripts). card_0062 shows the agent independently identifying both error class … [536 more characters]"}, {"id": "c39", "claim": "The agent left the $40M board restitution cap and any dollar restitution figure out of the application; the cap appears in the memo only.", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0073 shows the full draft application as written; its Section X (Restitution, ¶57) uses only general board-authorization language (\"where appropriate and as required by law\") with no $40M cap or any dollar figure. card_0075 shows the advisory memo containing the cap discussion — its open-items list explicitly asks to \"confirm the general-commitment language in ¶ 57 (no figure, no cap); separately, whether to take the cap question to the Board before filing\" — so the cap was kept in the memo only. card_0070 shows the drafting checklist decision (\"Restitution language (do not reference the $40M cap in the application)\") that drove this choice. card_0081 (verification notes over the round- … [277 more characters]"}, {"id": "c40", "claim": "The agent kept the Canadian Competition Bureau parallel application out of the DOJ application and raised it in the memo only.", "type": "decision", "section": 2, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0071 (the card the report passage cites) shows the agent writing this decision into its session notes: \"Canadian Competition Bureau parallel application: internal strategy only, kept OUT of the DOJ application.\" card_0083 shows the agent grepping the final draft-leniency-application.docx for \"Canadian Competition Bureau|Competition Bureau\" and finding 0 matches (\"no competition-bureau reference in app (good)\"). card_0087's final session-notes update confirms \"no Canadian Competition Bureau reference in application (kept in memo only),\" and card_0088's final-state description lists \"Canadian parallel application\" among the advisory memorandum's filing/process points, showing it was raise … [51 more characters]"}, {"id": "c41", "claim": "The final message says both documents were \"verified against all ten source documents\" (card 0088), but the records do not support that wording.", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim has two parts and both hold. (1) The final message does say the quoted wording: card_0088 (turn 91, last assistant message) contains verbatim \"Both deliverables are drafted, written to the requested paths, and verified against all ten source documents.\" (2) The records do not support that specific wording: the actual verification in the trace was term-based spot-checks and structural/round-trip checks, not a documented verification of both deliverables against each of the ten source documents. card_0077 and card_0079 show ~50 grep -c spot-checks of names/dates/figures against the documents' own text (verify-app.txt / verify-memo.txt); card_0083 shows docx zipfile validity, pandoc r … [385 more characters]"}, {"id": "c42", "claim": "Most per-document reads were elided (24 of 30), and the checks were grep presence tests and a self-review (cards 0077 to 0085).", "type": "verification_outcome", "section": 3, "verdict": "unsupported_or_contradicted", "why": "The claim has two parts and the second is wrong as stated. Part (a) — \"Most per-document reads were elided (24 of 30)\" — is consistent with the cards: exactly 24 of the source_inspection (per-document read) cards carry the \"[output truncated to 800 of N chars]\" elision marker (e.g. card_0077, card_0079, card_0085, and the earlier read cards), so the elided count checks out. Part (b) — \"the checks were grep presence tests and a self-review (cards 0077 to 0085)\" — mischaracterizes the range it names. The cards in 0077–0085 are not merely grep presence tests plus a self-review: card_0077/card_0079/card_0083/card_0085 are indeed grep presence/round-trip/docx-validity tests (grep -c of named term … [617 more characters]"}, {"id": "c43", "claim": "The DOJ Antitrust Division address in the application heading comes from the agent's own knowledge, not the sources (card 0080); the agent judged it standard, but it is unsourced.", "type": "decision", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0080 (the agent's self-review of the draft application) says verbatim: the heading \"United States Department of Justice, Antitrust Division, 450 Fifth Street, N.W., Washington, D.C. 20530\" is \"the standard DOJ Antitrust Division address (from my knowledge, not from the sources)\" and that \"the address isn't stated in the sources.\" This matches the claim on all three points: the address comes from the agent's own knowledge, the agent judged it standard/acceptable for a draft, and it is unsourced. A grep across all 88 cards confirms no source-document card contains the address — it appears only in the agent's own outputs (card_0085 shows it in the final heading, card_0080 in the agent's re … [9 more characters]"}, {"id": "c44", "claim": "Both documents use a March 2025 scenario calendar with a filing target of 10 March 2025, while the session notes record the environment date as 5 October 2026 (card 0071).", "type": "other", "section": 3, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0071 shows the session notes being written with \"Started 2026-10-05 (env date). Scenario timeline: target DOJ filing March 10, 2025\" — the environment date of 5 October 2026 as the claim cites card 0071 — and the decision \"Application dated Mar 10, 2025 (board target); memo dated Mar 6, 2025\", i.e. both deliverable documents on the March 2025 scenario calendar with a 10 March 2025 filing target. card_0070 corroborates: \"Today's date in the environment: 2026-10-05... target filing March 10, 2025\", with the application to be dated March 10, 2025 and the memo March 6, 2025."}, {"id": "c45", "claim": "The memo lists six open decisions, all due 7 March.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0075 shows the advisory memo's Section V, \"Open Items Requiring Your Decision (by March 7, 2025)\", listing exactly six numbered open decisions (personal-device demand, Czerny coverage posture, restitution, Canadian application, Exhibit A certification, approval of the Draft Application). card_0088's final-state summary independently describes the memo as containing \"six open decisions needed by March 7\", matching the claim's count and due date."}, {"id": "c46", "claim": "Czerny's individual coverage is undecided in the records.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The source records leave Czerny's individual coverage genuinely open. card_0061 (doj-leniency-policy-reference.txt) states that \"Holst and Vero are strong candidates for individual coverage\" while \"Czerny's coverage presents a genuine dilemma.\" card_0062 summarizes the record against the policy's coverage criteria: Czerny made only partial admissions, refused to turn over his personal phone, and has not committed to cooperating with DOJ, so he does not presently meet the admit/cooperate/no-obstruction requirements and no determination has been made. card_0073 (draft application ¶56(c)) reflects the same unresolved state: it reports his partial admissions and non-commitment and asks the Divis … [194 more characters]"}, {"id": "c47", "claim": "The $40M board restitution cap is below the $75.3M central estimate of CPS harm.", "type": "other", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0035 shows the board resolution authorizing restitution \"in an aggregate amount not to exceed $40,000,000\" without further board approval — the $40M board restitution cap. card_0062 records the verified figures: consumer harm total $287.8M with CPS's share $75.3M, derived from the central 12.3% overcharge estimate. card_0070 (the agent's own working notes) explicitly flags \"the gap between the $40M cap and the estimated $75.3M harm\" as a point to raise in the advisory memo. Both figures appear as stated and the comparison (40 < 75.3) holds."}, {"id": "c48", "claim": "Demands for personal-device data from Holst and Vero are unresolved.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0070 (the agent's synthesis of the internal investigation memo) lists as open evidence gaps that the \"Personal cell phones of Czerny, Holst, and Vero [are] not forensically imaged; ... negotiation ongoing\" and that \"Personal carrier call logs / SMS metadata [are] not yet obtained; may require subpoena.\" card_0075 (the advisory memo the agent drafted) carries an identical list in \"V. Open Items Requiring Your Decision,\" item 1: \"Formal personal-device demand to Czerny, Holst, and Vero, with stated consequences for litigation-hold non-compliance ... Do we issue it now?\" — i.e., as of the work's end the demand to Holst and Vero had not been resolved. card_0073 shows the same open-item list … [168 more characters]"}, {"id": "c49", "claim": "Source conflicts were recorded for the memo appendix, identified as card 0071.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0071 shows the agent writing /app/workspace/process/session-notes.md containing a section \"## Source discrepancies noted (to flag in memo appendix)\" with four enumerated source conflicts (account split, SignalVault group name, Czerny-Holst email span, Czerny title), and card_0072 confirms the write succeeded. So source conflicts intended for the memo appendix were indeed recorded at card 0071, matching the claim."}, {"id": "c50", "claim": "A February 18, 2022 SignalVault message splits the account as CPS 11, TriState 15, PacificPoly 12, SunCoast 9, while Kensington's Table 4 gives 11, 16, 12, 8 — a recorded conflict.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0070 records the agent's synthesis of the source documents: \"The Feb 18, 2022 SignalVault message has 'CPS 11, TriState 15, PacificPoly 12, SunCoast 9' = 47. Kensington Table 4: CPS 11, TriState 16, PacificPoly 12, SunCoast 8 = 47,\" flagged as a discrepancy between the two sources. That matches the claim's two splits (11/15/12/9 vs 11/16/12/8) exactly."}, {"id": "c51", "claim": "The group thread is named \"Industry Coordination\" in the transcripts but \"Industry Council\" in the notebook — a recorded conflict.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0070 records the discrepancy verbatim: the SignalVault transcript labels the group thread \"Industry Coordination\" (the forensic compilation, Bellwether & Hale, Feb 24, 2025), while the notebook's Nov 3, 2023 entry says \"SignalVault group name: 'Industry Council'\"; the agent explicitly flags it as a minor discrepancy and lists it as a source inconsistency. card_0075 shows the conflict carried into the deliverable: the advisory memo's \"Source Discrepancies Identified During Drafting\" item 2 records \"Industry Coordination\" (SignalVault compilation) vs. \"Industry Council\" (Czerny's notebook) and notes the draft resolved it by using \"Industry Coordination.\" The claim that this name mismatch … [86 more characters]"}, {"id": "c52", "claim": "The Czerny–Holst email date span is June 2022 to November 2024 in the investigation memo but March 2022 to December 2024 in the interview summary — a recorded conflict.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0019 (reading lines 330-483 of the internal-investigation-memo) shows the\nmemo's evidence list stating \"23 internal CPS emails between Czerny and Holst,\ndated between approximately June 2022 and November 2024.\" card_0031 (cat of\nczerny-interview-summary.txt) shows its Exhibit E listing the same 23 emails\nwith \"dates ranging from approximately March 2022 through December 2024.\"\ncard_0070's session notes independently flag this exact pair of ranges as a\ndocument discrepancy. The reported conflict — June 2022–November 2024 in the\ninvestigation memo vs. March 2022–December 2024 in the interview summary —\nmatches the source documents as stated."}, {"id": "c53", "claim": "Facing the conflicting Czerny–Holst email date spans, the agent used 2022–2024 in the report.", "type": "decision", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "The claim is a decision-type claim: the agent faced conflicting date spans for the 23 internal Czerny–Holst emails and resolved them by using \"2022–2024\" in the deliverables. card_0070 (working notes) records the conflict explicitly — \"approximately June 2022 – November 2024\" (investigation memo) vs. \"approximately March 2022 – December 2024\" (interview summary) — and the decision \"Use the vaguer '2022 – 2024.'\" card_0071 shows the same resolution persisted to process/session-notes.md: \"→ use vague '2022–2024'.\" card_0075 shows the advisory memo stating the draft \"uses the neutral '2022 through 2024,'\" and card_0073 (the draft application itself) contains the \"2022 through 2024\" phrasing, so … [54 more characters]"}, {"id": "c54", "claim": "The Kensington report once calls Czerny \"Senior VP of Sales\" while other sources say \"VP of Sales\" — a recorded conflict.", "type": "unresolved_work", "section": 4, "verdict": "supported", "why": "card_0040 shows the agent noticing and recording the title inconsistency while reading the sources: Czerny is \"VP of Sales for the Industrial Films Division in some sources, but Kensington refers to him as Senior Vice President of Sales.\" card_0070's discrepancy list records it as item 5 (\"Kensington report calls Czerny 'Senior Vice President of Sales' once, but other sources have 'Vice President of Sales'\") and notes the board resolutions, investigation memo, interview summary, and SignalVault key all use \"Vice President of Sales.\" card_0069 shows the investigation memo's \"Vice President of Sales\" wording for the competing sources, and card_0080's verification confirms the Kensington quote … [156 more characters]"}, {"id": "c55", "claim": "Every statement in the report is intended to trace to a card in cards/ (cited in parentheses), and the report uses only those records, noting full text is in cards/full/ for some cards only.", "type": "decision", "section": 4, "verdict": "unverifiable", "why": "The claim has two parts. The \"full text is in cards/full/ for some cards only\" part is visible in the saved evidence: a grep over all 88 cards shows 35 cards (e.g. card_0002, card_0007, card_0011) carrying \"output truncated to 800 of N chars; the full text is in cards/full/\" and 53 cards without it, so full text is indeed present for some cards only — consistent with the report's note. But the core of the claim is a statement of authorial intent and practice (\"Every statement in the report is intended to trace to a card,\" \"This report uses only those records\") that refers to the report as a whole, which the trace does not contain (only the cited passage is in hand). The saved cards show the … [240 more characters]"}], "recoveries": [{"event": "event_002", "verdict": "not_retried", "why": "card_0004 is the failed update_plan call, rejected at runtime with \"unsupported call: update_plan\". The agent's next reasoning (card_0006) states \"The update_plan tool is not available in this environment (I tried to call it but it wasn't supported). I'll proceed without it.\" and its next action (card_0005) is an unrelated exec_command (tool checks, mkdir). A grep over all 88 cards and all 9 chunks confirms update_plan is never called again — the agent abandoned that approach and never retried it."}, {"event": "event_027", "verdict": "adapted", "why": "card_0081 is the failure: the trailing `file` call in the edit/regeneration\ncommand exited 127 with \"/bin/bash: line 18: file: command not found\"\n(`file` is not installed), after the Python edits and both pandoc docx\nregenerations had already succeeded. The agent then retried the same\nfile-type validation goal with a different approach: per card_0082's\nreasoning (\"The `file` command doesn't exist... Let me verify the docx is\nvalid by checking it's a zip archive\"), card_0083 validates both docx files\nby opening them with Python's zipfile and asserting `word/document.xml` is\npresent, plus a pandoc round-trip, which passes (both files reported valid\ndocx). That is a changed, failure-relevant to … [30 more characters]"}]}, "branches": [], "reports": {"html": "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>Run report: CPS leniency drafting session<\/title>\n<style>\n:root{--ink:#1b1f24;--ink-2:#4a5260;--surface:#ffffff;--panel:#f3f5f8;--edge:#d3d8df;--accent:#1f5f8b;--hatch-bg:#d6e5f1}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){:root{--ink:#e8ebef;--ink-2:#aab2be;--surface:#14181d;--panel:#1c222a;--edge:#3a434f;--accent:#7fb3d9;--hatch-bg:#1d3347}}\n*{box-sizing:border-box}\nbody{margin:0;background:var(--surface);color:var(--ink);font:16px/1.55 system-ui,-apple-system,\"Segoe UI\",sans-serif}\nmain{max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;padding:32px 20px 64px}\nh1{font-size:1.7rem;margin:0 0 6px}\nh2{font-size:1.2rem;margin:36px 0 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);padding-bottom:4px}\np{margin:0 0 12px}\n.lede{font-size:1.05rem}\n.meta{color:var(--ink-2);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:8px}\nfigure{margin:20px 0;padding:16px;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--edge);border-radius:8px}\nfigure svg{width:100%;height:auto;display:block;font-family:system-ui,-apple-system,\"Segoe UI\",sans-serif}\nfigcaption{color:var(--ink-2);font-size:.92rem;margin-top:10px}\ntable{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin:10px 0 16px;font-size:.93rem}\nth,td{border-bottom:1px solid var(--edge);padding:6px 8px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}\nth{color:var(--ink-2);font-weight:600}\ntd.num{text-align:right;white-space:nowrap}\nul,ol{margin:0 0 12px;padding-left:22px}\nli{margin-bottom:6px}\ncode{font-size:.9em}\n.note{color:var(--ink-2);font-size:.92rem}\n<\/style>\n<\/head>\n<body>\n<main>\n\n<h1>Run report: CPS leniency drafting session<\/h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Covers the 88 records in cards/ (index at cards/index.md). Written from those records only.<\/p>\n\n<h2>Summary<\/h2>\n<p class=\"lede\">The agent prepared two Word documents for Consolidated Polymer Solutions, Inc. (CPS), drawn from ten source files: a draft U.S. Department of Justice Antitrust Division leniency application, and an advisory memorandum to Pamela Ng, General Counsel. The agent itself labels the application \"PROPOSED DRAFT ... NOT FOR FILING IN CURRENT FORM\" (card 0073). The records show no filing, sending, or signing.<\/p>\n<p>The original task prompt is not in the records, so the scope described here is taken from the deliverables and the agent's notes (card 0071). The agent's verification was partial: most source reads were cut off, and its final message claims more verification than the records support.<\/p>\n\n<h2>How the run unfolded<\/h2>\n<p>The run had six phases. Card numbers refer to the files in cards/.<\/p>\n\n<figure data-pil-kind=\"diagram\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 296\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Six phases of the agent's run<\/title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">Phase 1 Orient, cards 0001 to 0010. Phase 2 Read sources, cards 0011 to 0069. Phase 3 Synthesize, cards 0062 and 0070. Phase 4 Draft, cards 0071 to 0076. Phase 5 Verify, cards 0077 to 0085. Phase 6 Report, cards 0086 to 0088. Arrows show the order of the phases.<\/desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"arr\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\" style=\"fill:var(--ink-2)\"/><\/marker>\n<\/defs>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"16\" width=\"230\" height=\"104\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--edge)\"/>\n<text x=\"36\" y=\"42\" style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;fill:var(--ink)\">1  Orient<\/text>\n<text x=\"36\" y=\"63\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Listed 10 source files<\/text>\n<text x=\"36\" y=\"81\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">plan tool unsupported<\/text>\n<text x=\"36\" y=\"103\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">Cards 0001–0010<\/text>\n<rect x=\"315\" y=\"16\" width=\"230\" height=\"104\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--edge)\"/>\n<text x=\"331\" y=\"42\" style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;fill:var(--ink)\">2  Read sources<\/text>\n<text x=\"331\" y=\"63\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">30 per-document reads<\/text>\n<text x=\"331\" y=\"81\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">24 of 30 outputs elided<\/text>\n<text x=\"331\" y=\"103\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">Cards 0011–0069<\/text>\n<rect x=\"610\" y=\"16\" width=\"230\" height=\"104\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--edge)\"/>\n<text x=\"626\" y=\"42\" style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;fill:var(--ink)\">3  Synthesize<\/text>\n<text x=\"626\" y=\"63\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Conflicts and key figures<\/text>\n<text x=\"626\" y=\"81\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Long reasoning briefs<\/text>\n<text x=\"626\" y=\"103\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">Cards 0062, 0070<\/text>\n<rect x=\"610\" y=\"176\" width=\"230\" height=\"104\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--edge)\"/>\n<text x=\"626\" y=\"202\" style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;fill:var(--ink)\">4  Draft<\/text>\n<text x=\"626\" y=\"223\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Notes, application, memo<\/text>\n<text x=\"626\" y=\"241\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Markdown to two .docx files<\/text>\n<text x=\"626\" y=\"263\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">Cards 0071–0076<\/text>\n<rect x=\"315\" y=\"176\" width=\"230\" height=\"104\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--edge)\"/>\n<text x=\"331\" y=\"202\" style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;fill:var(--ink)\">5  Verify<\/text>\n<text x=\"331\" y=\"223\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Grep checks, self-review<\/text>\n<text x=\"331\" y=\"241\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Two edits; docx zip check<\/text>\n<text x=\"331\" y=\"263\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">Cards 0077–0085<\/text>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"176\" width=\"230\" height=\"104\" rx=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--surface);stroke:var(--edge)\"/>\n<text x=\"36\" y=\"202\" style=\"font-size:15px;font-weight:700;fill:var(--ink)\">6  Report<\/text>\n<text x=\"36\" y=\"223\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Notes updated; final message<\/text>\n<text x=\"36\" y=\"241\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Claims both files verified<\/text>\n<text x=\"36\" y=\"263\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">Cards 0086–0088<\/text>\n<line x1=\"254\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"311\" y2=\"68\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2);stroke-width:1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<line x1=\"549\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"606\" y2=\"68\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2);stroke-width:1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<line x1=\"725\" y1=\"122\" x2=\"725\" y2=\"172\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2);stroke-width:1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<line x1=\"606\" y1=\"228\" x2=\"549\" y2=\"228\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2);stroke-width:1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<line x1=\"311\" y1=\"228\" x2=\"254\" y2=\"228\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2);stroke-width:1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#arr)\"/>\n<\/svg>\n<figcaption>Figure 1. The six phases with the card ranges behind each. This is a sequence diagram, not measured data.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Orient (cards 0001–0010).<\/strong> The agent listed the workspace and found ten source files (card 0002). Its plan tool call returned \"unsupported call\" (card 0004), and it carried on without a plan. It checked its tools: pandoc was present, python-docx was not (card 0005). It converted the .docx sources to plain text under /tmp/work (card 0009).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Read sources (cards 0011–0069).<\/strong> The agent read the source documents one by one, mostly through pandoc output and cat. Details are in the next section.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Synthesize (cards 0062, 0070).<\/strong> Two long reasoning briefs recorded the key facts, the source conflicts, and the plan for the memo.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Draft (cards 0071–0076).<\/strong> The agent wrote process/session-notes.md (card 0071), wrote the application and memo as markdown (cards 0073, 0075), and converted both to .docx with pandoc (card 0075).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Verify (cards 0077–0085).<\/strong> Grep presence checks (cards 0077, 0079), a claim-by-claim self-review (card 0080), two edits and a regeneration (card 0081), a zip-level .docx check and pandoc round-trip (card 0083), and a heading check (card 0085).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Report (cards 0086–0088).<\/strong> The agent's reasoning states \"Both documents have been verified\" (card 0086). It added a verification section to the session notes (card 0087) and sent a final message (card 0088).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h2>What it read, and what it could not read in full<\/h2>\n<p>The records show 30 reads of individual source documents. Figure 2 counts them by document. A read counts as elided when its card shows truncated or cut-off output. A read that covers more than one document is counted under the first document it names. The first bulk read (card 0007) is not counted: it shows 800 characters of a 10,193-character output, and the pandoc output was capped at 700 lines.<\/p>\n\n<figure data-pil-kind=\"plot\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\">\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 392\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Source reads per document, with elided reads hatched<\/title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Horizontal stacked bars. Internal investigation memo: 7 reads, 6 elided. DOJ leniency policy reference: 7 reads, 5 elided. SignalVault transcripts: 4 reads, 3 elided. Kensington forensic report: 4 reads, 4 elided. Board resolutions: 3 reads, 2 elided. Czerny interview and Tanaka complaint: 3 reads, 3 elided. Czerny notebook excerpts: 2 reads, 1 elided. Total 30 reads, 24 elided.<\/desc>\n<defs>\n<pattern id=\"hatch\" width=\"6\" height=\"6\" patternUnits=\"userSpaceOnUse\" patternTransform=\"rotate(45)\"><rect width=\"6\" height=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--hatch-bg)\"/><rect width=\"3\" height=\"6\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\"/><\/pattern>\n<\/defs>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"14\" width=\"16\" height=\"12\" style=\"fill:var(--accent)\"/>\n<text x=\"292\" y=\"25\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Read, no truncation marker<\/text>\n<rect x=\"520\" y=\"14\" width=\"16\" height=\"12\" style=\"fill:url(#hatch);stroke:var(--accent)\"/>\n<text x=\"542\" y=\"25\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Output elided or cut off<\/text>\n\n<text x=\"0\" y=\"69\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Internal investigation memo<\/text>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"52\" width=\"60\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<rect x=\"330\" y=\"52\" width=\"360\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:url(#hatch);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"69\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">7 reads · 6 elided<\/text>\n\n<text x=\"0\" y=\"113\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">DOJ leniency policy reference<\/text>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"96\" width=\"120\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<rect x=\"390\" y=\"96\" width=\"300\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:url(#hatch);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"113\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">7 reads · 5 elided<\/text>\n\n<text x=\"0\" y=\"157\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">SignalVault transcripts<\/text>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"140\" width=\"60\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<rect x=\"330\" y=\"140\" width=\"180\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:url(#hatch);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"157\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">4 reads · 3 elided<\/text>\n\n<text x=\"0\" y=\"201\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Kensington forensic report<\/text>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"184\" width=\"240\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:url(#hatch);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"201\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">4 reads · 4 elided<\/text>\n\n<text x=\"0\" y=\"245\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Board resolutions<\/text>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"228\" width=\"60\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<rect x=\"330\" y=\"228\" width=\"120\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:url(#hatch);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"245\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">3 reads · 2 elided<\/text>\n\n<text x=\"0\" y=\"289\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Czerny interview, Tanaka complaint<\/text>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"272\" width=\"180\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:url(#hatch);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"289\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">3 reads · 3 elided<\/text>\n\n<text x=\"0\" y=\"333\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">Czerny notebook excerpts<\/text>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"316\" width=\"60\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:var(--accent);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<rect x=\"330\" y=\"316\" width=\"60\" height=\"24\" style=\"fill:url(#hatch);stroke:var(--panel);stroke-width:2\"/>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"333\" style=\"font-size:13px;fill:var(--ink)\">2 reads · 1 elided<\/text>\n\n<line x1=\"270\" y1=\"346\" x2=\"690\" y2=\"346\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2)\"/>\n<line x1=\"270\" y1=\"346\" x2=\"270\" y2=\"350\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2)\"/>\n<text x=\"270\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"font-size:12px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">0<\/text>\n<line x1=\"330\" y1=\"346\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"350\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2)\"/>\n<text x=\"330\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"font-size:12px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">1<\/text>\n<line x1=\"390\" y1=\"346\" x2=\"390\" y2=\"350\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2)\"/>\n<text x=\"390\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"font-size:12px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">2<\/text>\n<line x1=\"450\" y1=\"346\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"350\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2)\"/>\n<text x=\"450\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"font-size:12px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">3<\/text>\n<line x1=\"510\" y1=\"346\" x2=\"510\" y2=\"350\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2)\"/>\n<text x=\"510\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"font-size:12px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">4<\/text>\n<line x1=\"570\" y1=\"346\" x2=\"570\" y2=\"350\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2)\"/>\n<text x=\"570\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"font-size:12px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">5<\/text>\n<line x1=\"630\" y1=\"346\" x2=\"630\" y2=\"350\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2)\"/>\n<text x=\"630\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"font-size:12px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">6<\/text>\n<line x1=\"690\" y1=\"346\" x2=\"690\" y2=\"350\" style=\"stroke:var(--ink-2)\"/>\n<text x=\"690\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" style=\"font-size:12px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">7<\/text>\n<text x=\"270\" y=\"384\" style=\"font-size:12px;fill:var(--ink-2)\">Source reads (count of read commands)<\/text>\n<\/svg>\n<figcaption>Figure 2. Source reads per document in this run's records. Solid: read with no truncation marker. Hatched: output elided or cut off. Totals: 30 reads, 24 elided, 6 not elided.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Document<\/th><th class=\"num\">Reads<\/th><th class=\"num\">Elided<\/th><th class=\"num\">Not elided<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Internal investigation memo<\/td><td class=\"num\">7<\/td><td class=\"num\">6<\/td><td class=\"num\">1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>DOJ leniency policy reference<\/td><td class=\"num\">7<\/td><td class=\"num\">5<\/td><td class=\"num\">2<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>SignalVault transcripts<\/td><td class=\"num\">4<\/td><td class=\"num\">3<\/td><td class=\"num\">1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Kensington forensic report<\/td><td class=\"num\">4<\/td><td class=\"num\">4<\/td><td class=\"num\">0<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Board resolutions<\/td><td class=\"num\">3<\/td><td class=\"num\">2<\/td><td class=\"num\">1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Czerny interview summary and Tanaka complaint<\/td><td class=\"num\">3<\/td><td class=\"num\">3<\/td><td class=\"num\">0<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Czerny notebook excerpts<\/td><td class=\"num\">2<\/td><td class=\"num\">1<\/td><td class=\"num\">1<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><th>Total<\/th><th class=\"num\">30<\/th><th class=\"num\">24<\/th><th class=\"num\">6<\/th><\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n\n<p>Of the 35 full-text evidence files in cards/full, 25 contain a truncation or elision marker. Full text exists for some cards only.<\/p>\n<p>The session notes label the key figures \"verified\" (card 0071). Every read of the Kensington report in the cards is elided (4 of 4), so the figures it supplies rest on partial output unless the full-text files show more.<\/p>\n\n<h2>What it produced<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Draft leniency application<\/strong> (<code>/app/workspace/draft-leniency-application.docx<\/code>). Dated 10 March 2025, the board's target date. It has 58 numbered paragraphs, sections I to XI, Exhibits A and B, and a blank signature block. The Canadian parallel application is kept out of it (card 0071).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Advisory memorandum<\/strong> (<code>/app/workspace/advisory-memorandum.docx<\/code>). Dated 6 March 2025. Addressed to Pamela Ng, General Counsel, from Diana Vasquez-Koh and Nathan Prescott of Bellwether &amp; Hale LLP. Seven sections, six open decisions due 7 March, and an appendix of source discrepancies (card 0075).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Process notes<\/strong> (<code>/app/workspace/process/session-notes.md<\/code>). Source list, decisions, discrepancies, and a verification section (cards 0071, 0087).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h2>Choices the agent made<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>It used the names from the investigation memo, board resolutions, and transcripts, not from the internal policy reference's checklist. That checklist has name errors (TriState Polymers, SunCoast Materials, Luis Montez, Terrence Broussard) and region errors for Holst and Vero (card 0071). The corrected names are TriState Resin Corp., SunCoast Plastics, LLC, Javier Montez, and Annette Broussard.<\/li>\n<li>It left the $40M board restitution cap and any dollar restitution figure out of the application (card 0071). The cap appears in the memo only.<\/li>\n<li>It kept the Canadian Competition Bureau parallel application out of the DOJ application and raised it in the memo only (card 0071).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h2>What went wrong or was limited<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The plan tool returned \"unsupported call\" (card 0004). The agent continued without it.<\/li>\n<li>python-docx was not installed (card 0005). The agent wrote markdown and converted it with pandoc.<\/li>\n<li>Most source reads were partial (see above). Several cat outputs were cut off, and the agent split later reads by line range.<\/li>\n<li>The file command was missing (exit 127, card 0081). The agent validated the .docx files with a python zipfile check and a pandoc round-trip (card 0083).<\/li>\n<li>Grep checks for several full names found zero matches (cards 0077 to 0079). The agent attributed these to short-form usage (card 0078). The records do not show that explanation checked separately against the text.<\/li>\n<li>The self-review found a curly-apostrophe mismatch in \"Rossi's\", which the agent attributed to pandoc typography (card 0080).<\/li>\n<li>The self-review found that paragraph 50 claimed \"no contact since January 13,\" which was broader than the sources. The agent narrowed it (card 0081).<\/li>\n<li>A memo sentence attributed a Texas privacy-law point to Vero, which the sources do not support. It was rewritten to refer to Texas and Ohio state privacy law, since Mr. Holst is based in Ohio (card 0081).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<h2>What you should know before relying on the result<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li>The application is a draft. The agent itself marks it not for filing in its current form (card 0073). It needs legal review before any use.<\/li>\n<li>The final message says both documents were \"verified against all ten source documents\" (card 0088). The records do not support that wording. Most per-document reads were elided (24 of 30), and the checks were grep presence tests and a self-review (cards 0077 to 0085).<\/li>\n<li>The DOJ Antitrust Division address in the application heading comes from the agent's own knowledge, not the sources (card 0080). The agent judged it standard, but it is unsourced.<\/li>\n<li>The records show no check of the legal authorities or citations in either document.<\/li>\n<li>The Kensington figures come from a preliminary report (KFA-2025-0087, dated 24 February 2025).<\/li>\n<li>Both documents use a March 2025 scenario calendar, with a filing target of 10 March 2025. The session notes record the environment date as 5 October 2026 (card 0071). Dates, the 7 March decision deadline, and the memo's timing need to be reset before any use.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n\n<h2>Key figures used in the drafts<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Item<\/th><th>Figure as recorded<\/th><th>Where it appears in the records<\/th><\/tr><\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Affected commerce<\/td><td>$2.34B total; $612M for CPS<\/td><td>Kensington report (card 0071)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Average overcharge<\/td><td>12.3%<\/td><td>Kensington report (card 0071)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Consumer harm<\/td><td>$287.8M total; $75.3M for CPS<\/td><td>Kensington report (card 0071)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Treble exposure<\/td><td>$225.9M (range $183.6M to $275.4M)<\/td><td>Kensington report (card 0071)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Board restitution cap<\/td><td>$40M<\/td><td>Board resolutions (card 0071)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Price path<\/td><td>$0.82, $0.878, $0.922, $0.959 per lb (+16.95%)<\/td><td>Kensington report (card 0071)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Allocated accounts<\/td><td>47 total; CPS 11 (about $68M a year)<\/td><td>Session notes (card 0071); card 0070<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Bids<\/td><td>342 total; 127 no-bids; 34 courtesy bids<\/td><td>Session notes (card 0071)<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>SignalVault messages<\/td><td>214 recovered; 90-day auto-deletion; earlier messages lost<\/td><td>Card 0070<\/td><\/tr>\n<tr><td>Prior matter<\/td><td>2014 consent decree, 4:14-cv-02891 (S.D. Tex.), expired 12 September 2019<\/td><td>Session notes (card 0071)<\/td><\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p class=\"note\">These are the figures as the agent recorded them. This report did not recompute them or check them against full-text source files.<\/p>\n\n<h2>Open points<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The memo lists six open decisions due 7 March.<\/li>\n<li>Czerny's individual coverage is undecided in the records.<\/li>\n<li>The $40M board restitution cap is below the $75.3M central estimate of CPS harm.<\/li>\n<li>Demands for personal-device data from Holst and Vero are unresolved.<\/li>\n<li>Source conflicts recorded for the memo appendix (card 0071):\n<ul>\n<li>Account split: a February 18, 2022 SignalVault message gives CPS 11, TriState 15, PacificPoly 12, SunCoast 9. Kensington's Table 4 gives 11, 16, 12, 8.<\/li>\n<li>Group thread name: \"Industry Coordination\" in the transcripts; \"Industry Council\" in the notebook.<\/li>\n<li>Czerny–Holst email date span: June 2022 to November 2024 in the investigation memo; March 2022 to December 2024 in the interview summary. The agent used 2022–2024.<\/li>\n<li>The Kensington report once calls Czerny \"Senior VP of Sales\"; other sources say \"VP of Sales\".<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n\n<p class=\"note\">Every statement above traces to a card in cards/, cited in parentheses. Full text is in cards/full/ for some cards only. This report uses only those records.<\/p>\n\n<\/main>\n<\/body>\n<\/html>\n"}, "same_run": "harvey-labs--codex--antitrust-competition-draft-leniency-application"});
